window.BIZ_PAPERS = [{"id":"U6-1801","unit":6,"key":"2018-01","series":"January 2018","title":"NBCC — construction (human resources)","theme":"Human resource management","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for the HR Director to present to the Board of Directors at NBCC. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of what human resource planning involves and its importance to NBCC • make recommendations about the key issues to be considered by NBCC to maintain its competitiveness and continuing success. The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to NBCC’s Board of Directors. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the different approaches to training and development available to NBCC • make recommendations about how NBCC can measure the effectiveness of training and development undertaken by its staff.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Sector and company background information The construction sector Construction is one of the largest sectors in the UK economy. In 2013 it comprised over 280,000 businesses with approximately 2.9 million employees. The UK construction sector has been a key driver of economic growth. However, it has been particularly vulnerable to changes in the external environment. The 2008 recession had a major impact on the sector with its contribution to the UK’s value of goods and services produced falling from 8.9% in 2007 to 6.7% in 2011."},{"t":"p","x":"The UK construction industry remains one of the largest in Europe. There has been a growing demand for construction, both within the UK and globally. The UK has a good international reputation for construction services, especially for architecture and the development of advanced construction technologies."},{"t":"p","x":"The global construction market is transforming as it continues to move into green and sustainable construction, maximising opportunities presented by the digital economy. If the UK construction industry is to continue to take a leading role in this transformation it must be both ready for emerging challenges and, at the same time, maintain its competitiveness."},{"t":"p","x":"A major factor that can affect the competitiveness of businesses within the construction sector is the skills and flexibility of the workforce. The situation in the UK construction workforce is mixed. The proportion of people in the sector holding higher level qualifications continues to increase. However, there have been problems both with apprenticeships not being completed and in some cases the right apprenticeships not being available. Additionally, there are many vacancies that employers find difficult to fill with staff who have the correct skills, qualifications and/or experience. The industry has many self-employed workers and there continues to be little sustained training and development amongst the self-employed."},{"t":"p","x":"The UK construction industry is made up of three sub-sectors: 1. Contracting – the construction of buildings, civil engineering 2. Services – architectural, quantity surveying, wholesaling of construction materials, plumbing and heating equipment, renting and leasing of equipment 3. Products – manufacture of construction products and materials. Table 1: UK construction industry productivity change and profitability 2008–2016"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Year 2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016","Productivity","% change 4.0 –2.0 10.0 23.0 1.5 2.0 1.5 0.5 0.5","Profitability","% 9.6 9.9 7.5 5.0 3.0 2.0 2.1 2.8 2.8"]},{"t":"p","x":"Nugent and Barton Construction Company Ltd Nugent and Barton Construction Company Ltd (NBCC) is a housebuilder and general construction company based in the West Midlands. It works within the contracting sub-sector of the UK construction industry."},{"t":"p","x":"NBCC was set up in 1975 and has grown into an award-winning company that has won the best medium-sized Housebuilder of the Year Award three times. In 2017 a new Managing Director was appointed following the retirement of the previous post holder. Some of the Directors also retired and there are a number of new appointments to the Board of Directors."},{"t":"p","x":"For NBCC to continue to be successful it needs to focus on maintaining a skilled and flexible workforce. This requires an effective human resource planning system which will allow NBCC to anticipate its labour needs and so meet its customers’ demands effectively. Part of human resource planning includes developing the skills, talents and potential of the existing staff. When vacancies arise NBCC is committed to attracting skilled and qualified young people and recruiting the best candidates available. It continues to invest in the training and development of these new staff and its existing employees. It does not currently employ apprentices but has done so in the past. NBCC has a young workforce and a much lower turnover of staff than is the average within the contracting sub-sector and the construction industry as a whole."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Table 2: Age distribution of NBCC staff","Age Staff %","16–25 18","26–40 38","41–50 24","51–65 20"]},{"t":"tbl","x":["Table 3: UK construction industry staff turnover rates (%)","Averages for construction sector and contracting sub-sector 2008–2016","2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016","Construction","sector 6.4 4.8 2.4 3.3 2.5 3.0 4.0 5.0 5.0","Contracting","sub-sector 9.6 6.3 4.8 6.2 5.8 6.1 7.0 8.1 8.0","NBCC staff turnover rates (%)","2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016","NBCC 4.6 3.3 2.1 3.0 2.9 3.0 4.2 4.9 4.9"]},{"t":"p","x":"NBCC has always invested in its staff by providing training and development opportunities to employees at all levels, from young employees to senior managers. The purpose of the training programme is to develop employees’ existing skills or for them to gain new ones, and is related to their existing work role. The development programme is about helping individuals to achieve their full potential regardless of whether this is related to their current job role."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Table 4: NBCC data","Net income £3.24 million","Staff training days","(average 2012–2017) 600","Training programme","% of staff","(average 2012–2017)","15%"]},{"t":"p","x":"As changes in the external environment and the need to be increasingly competitive impact on NBCC, the Board of Directors is going to discuss the effectiveness of the training and development programme. The Board recognises that while training and development brings benefits to both the business and the employee, it is a major cost to the company."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for the HR Director to present to the Board of Directors at NBCC. Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of what human resource planning involves and its importance to NBCC","make recommendations about the key issues to be considered by NBCC to maintain its competitiveness and continuing success."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to NBCC’s Board of Directors. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script. The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the different approaches to training and development available to NBCC","make recommendations about how NBCC can measure the effectiveness of training and development undertaken by its staff. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The construction sector and company background information Construction is one of the largest sectors in the UK economy. In 2013 it comprised over 280,000 businesses with approximately 2.9 million employees. The UK construction sector has been a key driver of economic growth. However, it has been particularly vulnerable to changes in the external environment. The 2008 recession had a major impact on the sector with its contribution to the UK’s value of goods and services produced falling from 8.9% in 2007 to 6.7% in 2011."},{"t":"p","x":"A major factor which can affect the competitiveness of businesses within the construction sector is the skills and flexibility of the workforce. The situation in the UK construction workforce is mixed. The proportion of people in the sector holding higher level qualifications continues to increase. However, there have been problems both with apprenticeships not being completed and in some cases the right apprenticeships not being available. Additionally, there are many vacancies which employers find difficult to fill with staff who have the correct skills, qualifications and/or experience. The industry has many self-employed workers and there continues to be little sustained training and development amongst the self-employed."},{"t":"p","x":"6 Nugent and Barton Construction Company Ltd Nugent and Barton Construction Company Ltd (NBCC) is a housebuilder and general construction company based in the West Midlands. It works within the contracting sub-sector of the UK construction industry."},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"","c":["7","Table 3:","UK","Construction industry staff turnover rates (%)","Average for construction sector and contracting sub-sector 2008–2016","2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016","Construction sector 6.4 4.8 2.4 3.3 2.5 3.0 4.0 5.0 5.0","Contracting sub-sector 9.6 6.3 4.8 6.2 5.8 6.1 7.0 8.1 8.0","NBCC staff turnover rates (%)"],"v":["2008","2009","2010","2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016"]}},{"t":"p","x":"NBCC 4.6 3.3 2.1 3.0 2.9 3.0 4.2 4.9 4.9"},{"t":"p","x":"8 NBCC has always invested in its staff by providing training and development opportunities to employees at all levels, from young employees to senior managers. The purpose of the training programme is to develop employees’ existing skills or to gain new ones, and is related to their existing work role. The development programme is about helping individuals to achieve their full potential regardless whether or not this is related to their current job role."}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-1806","unit":6,"key":"2018-06","series":"June 2018","title":"Strines Stoves Ltd (SSLtd)","theme":"Quality management","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for Chris North to present to the Board of Directors at SSLtd. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of quality management and its importance to SSLtd\n• make recommendations about the appropriate techniques or tools that SSLtd could use for quality management. The report should be word processed and in a minimum size 12-point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given by Chris North to SSLtd’s Board of Directors. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of how the current management approach is affecting the development of a quality culture at SSLtd\n• make recommendations about how SSLtd could achieve a quality management standard.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Strines Stoves Ltd (SSLtd) The Domestic Heating Market The UK domestic heating sector is substantial, highly competitive and covers a number of markets. One market is the manufacture of products such as boilers, stoves and ranges. This market is influenced by a number of factors such as:"},{"t":"li","x":["fuel prices","energy efficiency legislation","renewable technologies","other technological developments, e.g. smart meters and thermostats, next-gen insulation","trends in housebuilding and home improvement."]},{"t":"p","x":"UK householders are becoming more environmentally aware and are increasingly looking to smart heating solutions as a way to minimise energy usage and to save money. There is a renewed interest among consumers in burning wood for heating, with multi-fuel stoves being the most popular choice."},{"t":"p","x":"Strines Stoves Ltd (SSLtd) has been manufacturing metal multi-fuel stoves since the business was set up in 1985. Since the business was started SSLtd’s production processes have not changed. Currently the production staff are organised in teams of 15 with one supervisor per team. Production managers give instructions to supervisors who pass these on to individual team members to direct their work. Supervisors also record how many units have been completed or part finished each day, passing this information on to the production managers. The workplace is chaotic with metal panels and other components cluttering the production area."},{"t":"p","x":"Chris North has recently been appointed Operations Manager. His last job was in the automotive sector. Chris has found that the production processes at SSLtd need to change. He is a proactive manager and wants to improve and update operations. He wants to increase staff involvement and contribution to the production process. Chris would like to improve quality management by introducing a lean manufacturing approach similar to that used by Jaguar Land Rover Automotive plc and other successful businesses. He wants to develop a quality culture at SSLtd and achieve an appropriate quality standard."},{"t":"p","x":"The Chief Executive Officer (CEO) of SSLtd has identified the need to maintain a competitive advantage. He would like to develop a quality culture and introduce new production methods."},{"t":"p","x":"The CEO supports Chris’s target of achieving a quality standard, but other members of the Board of Directors are happy with the way things are. They state that the business has survived in a competitive market, making a small profit each year. They also claim that the workforce seems happy because there is a low staff turnover."},{"t":"p","x":"Jaguar Land Rover Automotive plc’s Manufacturing Strategy Jaguar Land Rover Automotive plc (JLR), part of Tata Motors, is the largest automotive manufacturer in the UK. In the 2016 Bloomberg survey of the country’s 400 largest employers, JLR was voted the ‘Best Employer to Work For in the UK’ . JLR’s strategy for long-term sustainable growth is built on its three principles:"},{"t":"li","x":["Customer First"]},{"t":"p","x":"Customer First has resulted in the company listening to its customers. In 2015/16 the company created an ‘always on’ real-time customer listening panel. This principle means that everyone in the company must aim to satisfy internal and external customers by understanding their needs and exceeding their expectations."},{"t":"li","x":["Great Products"]},{"t":"p","x":"JLR produces a range of distinctive award-winning vehicles."},{"t":"li","x":["Environmental Innovation"]},{"t":"p","x":"Environmental Innovation includes REALCAR, an initiative in which waste aluminium scrap from JLR vehicle production is returned to the supplier and recycled into aluminium sheets for use in the manufacture of future JLR cars. There are other recycling and waste reduction initiatives."},{"t":"p","x":"In JLR’s quality culture every employee is invited to contribute ideas to improve the way the company works, eliminate waste and create efficiencies. The company has implemented Team Improvement Circles to increase efficiency by listening to its employees."},{"t":"p","x":"Lean manufacturing techniques include organising the workforce into small teams with a supporting Team Leader. JLR operates a Just in Time continuous flow production process with visible control management boards."},{"t":"tbl","x":["JLR 2012–16","2012 2013 2014 2015 2016","Revenue (£m) 13 512 15 784 19 386 21 866 22 208","Profit pre-tax (£m) 1 479 1 674 2 501 2 614 1 557","(Source: figures adapted from JLR Annual Report 2015–16)","Figure 1","JLR Sales by Region 2015–161","UK N America China2 Europe Rest of the","world","Sales (%) 20 19 19 24 18","Change in sales","from 2014–15 (%) +24 +27 –16 +24 0","1 521 571 units sold in 2015–16","2 Adverse impact of slowdown in the Chinese economy","(Source: figures adapted from JLR Annual Report 2015–16)","Figure 2","JLR – Other Highlights 2015–16","1 Voted Number 1 in Bloomberg’s ‘Best Employers UK 2016’","2 Exported 80% of all vehicles produced"]},{"t":"p","x":"3 Employed 3 000 new recruits (including 600 graduates) increasing workforce to 38 000 worldwide 4 Won 150 awards including the Queen’s Award for Sustainable Development 5 Invested £3.1bn in the company"},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 3"},{"t":"p","x":"Lean Manufacturing Techniques in the Service Industries Lean manufacturing techniques have been adapted for use in the service industries. The supermarket chain, Aldi, applies a lean approach to its business operations, including:"},{"t":"li","x":["Just in Time approach to stock control","staff trained to carry out a range of different tasks/jobs in the store","stocking fewer variations of each product compared to other UK supermarkets","trolleys having a £1 deposit system","rejecting 24 hour a day opening","sourcing the majority of its fruit and vegetables locally","customers packing shopping away from the check-out","pallet-based displays","replacing standard practices such as shelf stacking by topping up individual products using display-ready consignments."]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for Chris North to present to the Board of Directors at SSLtd. Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of quality management and its importance to SSLtd","make recommendations about the appropriate techniques or tools that SSLtd could use for quality management."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in a minimum size 12-point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given by Chris North to SSLtd’s Board of Directors. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script. The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of how the current management approach is affecting the development of a quality culture at SSLtd","make recommendations about how SSLtd could achieve a quality management standard. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Strines Stoves Ltd The Domestic Heating Market The UK domestic heating sector is substantial, highly competitive and covers a number of markets. One market is the manufacture of products such as boilers, stoves and ranges. This market is influenced by a number of factors such as:"},{"t":"p","x":"7 Jaguar Land Rover Automotive plc’s Manufacturing Strategy Jaguar Land Rover Automotive plc (JLR), part of Tata Motors, is the largest automotive manufacturer in the UK. In the 2016 Bloomberg survey of the country’s 400 largest employers, JLR was voted the ‘Best Employer to Work For in the UK’ . JLR’s strategy for long-term sustainable growth is built on its three principles:"},{"t":"li","x":["Customer First"]},{"t":"li","x":["Great Products"]},{"t":"p","x":"JLR produces a range of distinctive award-winning vehicles."},{"t":"li","x":["Environmental Innovation"]},{"t":"tbl","x":["8","JLR 2012–16","2012 2013 2014 2015 2016","Revenue","(£m) 13 512 15 784 19 386 21 866 22 208","Profit","pre-tax (£m) 1 479 1 674 2 501 2 614 1 557","(Source: figures adapted from JLR Annual Report 2015–16)","Figure 1","JLR Sales by Region 2015–161","UK N America China2 Europe Rest of the","world","Sales (%) 20 19 19 24 18","Change in sales","from 2014–15 (%) +24 +27 -16 +24 0","1 521 571 units sold in 2015–16","2 Adverse impact of slowdown in the Chinese economy","(Source: figures adapted from JLR Annual Report 2015–16)","Figure 2","JLR – Other Highlights 2015–16","1 Voted Number 1 in Bloomberg’s ‘Best Employers UK 2016’","2 Exported 80% of all vehicles produced"]},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 3"},{"t":"p","x":"9 Lean Manufacturing Techniques in the Service Industries Lean manufacturing techniques have been adapted for use in the service industries. The supermarket chain, Aldi, applies a lean approach to its business operations, including:"}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-1901","unit":6,"key":"2019-01","series":"January 2019","title":"Anvil Cloud Ltd","theme":"Management and leadership; HR","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager to give to the owners of Anvil Cloud Ltd. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of the need to manage motivation and the employee experience at Anvil Cloud Ltd to ensure business success\n• recommend the actions to be taken to have effective financial and non-financial motivators at Anvil Cloud Ltd. The report should be word processed and in size 12 point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to the owners of Anvil Cloud Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the skills requirement issues that face management and leadership at Anvil Cloud Ltd\n• make recommendations on the techniques that can be used to meet skills requirements at Anvil Cloud Ltd.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Employee Engagement Google has a worldwide workforce of over 50 000 employees. Since Google was founded in 1998 it has frequently held the top spot on the Fortune 100 ‘Best Company to Work For’ list."},{"t":"p","x":"Google’s vision is ‘To create the happiest, most productive workplace in the world’ . This is reflected in its motivational model. Google’s Vice-President of People Development has said that Google wants its employees and future employees to love working there because that is what is going to make Google successful. Google considers its employees to be its biggest asset."},{"t":"p","x":"Like some other multinational companies, Google offers employees both financial and non-financial motivators. These motivators include health and dental insurance, workplace pensions, and paid-for training and development tuition. Google also offers:"},{"t":"li","x":["free lunch and dinner every day","on-site doctors","free fitness centres","financial support for adoption","maternity/paternity benefits including free takeaway meals for three months after child birth."]},{"t":"p","x":"Employees are encouraged to share their ideas and thoughts, and to participate in the company. There are regular surveys and employee forums to collect their views. Google offers its employees financial benefits as well as many more non-financial benefits."},{"t":"p","x":"Despite Google’s success, many businesses do not take a similar approach to providing such benefits for their employees."},{"t":"p","x":"In the UK, workplace pensions are now compulsory for any business employing staff and there is also legislation concerning maximum hours of work and paid holiday entitlement. However, additional motivational benefits are not provided by many organisations."},{"t":"p","x":"There are many advantages for employers and organisations in motivating their employees and increasing employee engagement. The ‘Employee Experience’ is now the strategy being developed by many forward-thinking organisations rather than relying on narrow engagement strategies."},{"t":"p","x":"The white paper: ‘Engaging Generation Me’ is a research paper produced by Management Today and Sopra Steria. The research identified that over half of Chief Executive Officers (CEOs) in the survey admitted that their organisations cared less about the employee experience than the quality of the customer experience."},{"t":"p","x":"The research also identified that two-thirds of the CEOs had not given specific accountability for employee experience to anyone, or appointed a Head of Employee Experience. The findings also showed that more than half of the CEOs did not have a clear employee experience strategy in place. The results of the survey are shown in Figure 1."},{"t":"tbl","x":["0","5","10","15","20","25","30","35","Executive","Board","HR","Department","No-one Don't know Each office Other","department"]},{"t":"p","x":"Survey responses for the question: ‘Who is accountable for the specific task of improving the employee experience?’ % of responses (Source: figures adapted from White Paper: Engaging Generation Me)"},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Anvil Cloud Ltd Anvil Cloud Ltd is a computer support provider specialising in cloud-based IT solutions for UK businesses. Its solutions allow users to work collaboratively anywhere, anytime, in real time. It operates in a highly competitive market. The business was started in 2012. The majority of its clients are growing small and medium-sized businesses but it also has some national Public Limited Company (PLC) accounts. Each of its clients has a need to work collaboratively and often remotely with their employees and other businesses. Anvil Cloud Ltd’s solutions allow its clients to do this on any device, giving them the freedom to work anywhere, anytime. Their data is safe and protected to ISO standards. The service that Anvil Cloud Ltd offers does not require its clients to have expensive servers. This means that clients do not have to invest constantly in new computer hardware. Anvil Cloud Ltd is based in Reading in the Creative Studios in the town centre, near to a good range of amenities. It is a modern, open-plan flexible workspace. There is room for Anvil Cloud Ltd to grow."},{"t":"p","x":"The founder of Anvil Cloud Ltd, Pam Grainer, wants to expand the workforce immediately. She plans to recruit 10 new members of staff as Infrastructure Specialists and Front End Developers to deal with the new contracts that Anvil Cloud Ltd has won. The future workforce at Anvil Cloud Ltd will be a total of 80 employees after the recruitment. The current structure of its workforce and salary ranges for each role are shown in Figure 2. Current structure of the workforce and salaries at Anvil Cloud Ltd"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Job role Number","employed","Salary ranges for","each role","(£)","Front End Developer 6 60 000 – 70 000","Infrastructure Specialist 6 50 000 – 70 000","Encryption Specialist 6 50 000 – 70 000","Technical Consultant 10 40 000 – 60 000","Senior Web/ Graphic Designer 10 45 000 – 50 000","Digital Graphic Designer 10 30 000 – 45 000","Junior Digital Graphic Designer 10 25 000 – 35 000","Account Manager 4 25 000 – 35 000","Intern 8 0","Total 70","Figure 2"]},{"t":"tbl","x":["The staff turnover is shown in Figure 3.","Staff turnover rate at Anvil Cloud Ltd","Job role Number","employed","Average annual","staff turnover rate","(%)","Front End Developer 6 33","Infrastructure Specialist 6 33","Encryption Specialist 6 33","Technical Consultant 10 40","Senior Web/Graphic Designer 10 50","Digital Graphic Designer 10 40","Junior Digital Graphic Designer 10 60","Account Manager 4 25","Intern 8 100","Figure 3"]},{"t":"p","x":"The market is highly competitive with high demand for well-trained and experienced staff in this industry. Demand outweighs supply and employers compete aggressively for staff. Anvil Cloud Ltd knows that there is high demand and that it must compete to recruit and retain staff."},{"t":"p","x":"Anvil Cloud Ltd recruits interns for a period of six months to one year. During this period an intern learns about the technology and the basics of different roles, moving between departments. They learn how to work with clients and meet their needs. The owners of Anvil Cloud Ltd know that the employee experience is extremely important for helping to retain staff. They have looked at Google and other multinational organisations who are leading in the field of the employee experience, and who recognise that employees are their biggest asset."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager to give to the owners of Anvil Cloud Ltd. Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the need to manage motivation and the employee experience at Anvil Cloud Ltd to ensure business success","recommend the actions to be taken to have effective financial and non-financial motivators at Anvil Cloud Ltd."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12 point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to the owners of Anvil Cloud Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script."},{"t":"p","x":"The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the skills requirement issues that face management and leadership at Anvil Cloud Ltd","make recommendations on the techniques that can be used to meet skills requirements at Anvil Cloud Ltd. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"7 Despite Google’s success, many businesses do not take a similar approach to providing such benefits for their employees."},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 1"},{"t":"p","x":"8 Anvil Cloud Ltd Anvil Cloud Ltd is a computer support provider specialising in cloud-based IT solutions for UK businesses. Its solutions allow users to work collaboratively anywhere, anytime, in real time. It operates in a highly competitive market. The business was started in 2012. The majority of its clients are growing small and medium-sized businesses but it also has some national Public Limited Company (PLC) accounts. Each of its clients has a need to work collaboratively and often remotely with their employees and other businesses. Anvil Cloud Ltd’s solutions allow its clients to do this on any device, giving them the freedom to work anywhere, anytime. Their data is safe and protected to ISO standards. The service that Anvil Cloud Ltd offers does not require its clients to have expensive servers. This means that clients do not have to invest constantly in new computer hardware. Anvil Cloud Ltd is based in Reading in the Creative Studios in the town centre, near to a good range of amenities. It is a modern, open-plan flexible workspace. There is room for Anvil Cloud Ltd to grow."},{"t":"tbl","x":["9","The staff turnover is shown in Figure 3.","Staff turnover rate at Anvil Cloud Ltd","Job role Number","employed","Average annual","staff turnover rate","(%)","Front End Developer 6 33","Infrastructure Specialist 6 33","Encryption Specialist 6 33","Technical Consultant 10 40","Senior Web/Graphic Designer 10 50","Digital Graphic Designer 10 40","Junior Digital Graphic Designer 10 60","Account Manager 4 25","Intern 8 100","Figure 3"]}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-1906","unit":6,"key":"2019-06","series":"June 2019","title":"Cookson Chemist Ltd (CCL)","theme":"Leadership and change","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager to give to the owners of Cookson Chemist Ltd (CCL). Your report should:\n• show your understanding of the need to manage the proposed change at CCL successfully, including the factors and stakeholders that will influence this change\n• make recommendations about the actions CCL needs to take to manage the proposed change.\nThe report should be word processed and in a minimum size 12-point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation for your manager to give to the owners of CCL . The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the purpose, types and impact of performance appraisals that CCL could use\n• recommend improvements that CCL could make in its performance appraisal processes.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Change Organisational change occurs for a number of internal and external reasons. These include takeovers and mergers, changes of leadership, new technology, high staff turnover and business performance. Change is not always successful. Research into initiatives that aim to deliver organisational change demonstrates that such initiatives have varied results. One survey found that only 25% of such initiatives relating to organisational change succeeded over the long term. Other research quotes success rates of similar initiatives ranging from 55% to 70%. However, management experts all agree that successful change relies on effective planning. Change is necessary if a business is to continue to operate and be successful. However, organisational change, in particular, causes a lot of anxiety for businesses, including management, employees and other stakeholders. Organisational change will fail if not planned and managed effectively. The best outcomes result in organisations that meet the objectives of all their stakeholders."},{"t":"p","x":"Employees often resist change, usually because they are afraid of the unknown and the possibility of losing their jobs and income. Research into change in one large organisation asked employees to say whether or not certain factors influenced their response to change. Some of the outcomes of this research are given in Figure 1. Factors influencing employees’ response to change"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Factor Yes No Don’t","know","%","I understand the changes that I am being asked to make 50 44 6","I agree with the changes 35 39 26","I feel I need to protect my colleagues 65 15 20","I have had poor experience of past changes 40 53 7","There are too many changes 49 47 4","Figure 1"]},{"t":"p","x":"To be successful, organisational change must involve employees from the start of the planning process."},{"t":"p","x":"Organisations often plan to reduce the workforce when implementing change. Making staff redundant is not an automatic outcome of organisational change. There have been examples of successful change without a reduction in the number of employees. Owners and employers must consider whether or not reducing staff numbers will make the company more efficient."},{"t":"p","x":"In many of the cases where roles became redundant there were no cuts in staff numbers because there was redeployment within the organisation. Cutting staff does not always"},{"t":"p","x":"result in a reduction in costs. One of the other impacts of redundancy is that it can also reduce motivation. Employees often say that they like their job because of the people they work with and, if these people leave, it will impact on the motivation of those who remain."},{"t":"p","x":"Research into organisational change that resulted in redundancies showed that the change was not always successful. Some of the outcomes of this research are given in"},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 2."},{"t":"p","x":"Outcomes of organisational change that resulted in redundancies Costs of redundancies and change management were often greater than salary savings 56% of employees reported a loss of motivation following redundancies 51% of employees reported doubts about whether the correct people were chosen for redundancy 48% of employees reported a loss of respect for their employer Productivity increased in 20% of organisations surveyed 65% of employees reported that the change was badly communicated to them BUT 63% of employers/owners reported that the change was clearly communicated to employees"},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 2"},{"t":"p","x":"When deciding which staff will go into a redundancy pool, organisations use data on staff performance, skills and expertise. This information will nearly always be collected at performance appraisal. However, many employees say they are unclear about the reasons for the choice of people who are made redundant."},{"t":"p","x":"There have been recent changes in the way a number of organisations, both multinationals and small and medium-sized organisations, conduct their performance appraisals. There is a growing movement away from annual performance appraisals towards the development of continuous performance management. This includes weekly ‘check-ins’ and ‘on-the-job conversations’ . Organisations leading the way in changing performance appraisal include Adobe, Deloitte and Google."},{"t":"p","x":"Some organisations are combining regular ‘on-the-job conversations’ with less structured annual reviews. General Electric is one of these organisations and calls these conversations ‘touchpoints’."},{"t":"p","x":"Organisations that have changed their approach to performance appraisal have reported increased productivity and performance. Adobe reported a decrease of 30% in voluntary staff turnover."},{"t":"p","x":"Cookson Chemist Ltd (CCL) CCL is a small regional chain of chemist shops with its headquarters in Shirevale. CCL was originally set up by James and Victoria Cookson. Its shares are now owned by family and friends of Nick Cookson, the current Managing Director. Two other directors manage the company with Nick."},{"t":"p","x":"Over an 18-month period CCL expanded by taking over independent chemist shops in local towns and villages. CCL now has 11 shops including the main shop in Shirevale. As part of the expansion plans an online prescription ordering service was introduced. A prescription delivery service to customers’ homes or local shops was introduced at the same time. This service is run from C C L’s headquarters in Shirevale. The expansion through takeovers and the introduction of the online prescription ordering service resulted in restructuring changes within CCL . These included:"},{"t":"li","x":["a reduction in the number of senior pharmacists","a reduction in the number of shop manager roles","a reduction in the number of shop assistant roles","an increase in the number of pharmacists","an increase in the number of delivery driver roles","the introduction of one senior pharmacist based in the Shirevale shop","the introduction of one deputy senior pharmacist based in the Shirevale shop."]},{"t":"p","x":"Each of the other 10 shops now has one pharmacist. The larger shops will also have a shop manager. In the smaller shops, the pharmacist will also act as the shop manager. At the time of the restructuring, the directors of CCL used the information from the previous two annual performance appraisals to help them decide which staff would be made redundant and/or offered redeployment within the company. This approach was problematic because of the way the performance appraisals had been implemented. Problems included:"},{"t":"li","x":["some appraisal records were missing","some appraisal records were not fully complete","some appraisal records were more detailed than others","some employees had not received any feedback after the appraisal","there was an inconsistent approach to implementing the appraisal process by managers","employees approached the performance appraisal process differently."]},{"t":"p","x":"The restructuring was not popular with the employees. They felt that the reasons for the change, the need for the reorganisation of staff and the final staffing structure were badly communicated to them."},{"t":"p","x":"Nick Cookson and his two fellow directors thought that the letters sent to staff and the notices on the staff notice boards in each shop clearly communicated the reasons for the reorganisation and the new structure that was to be put in place. The restructuring process caused a period of uncertainty among staff. The staff also experienced a loss of motivation and a loss of trust in Nick Cookson and his two fellow directors. There was an increase in voluntary staff turnover, including the loss of pharmacists. CCL then had unexpected recruitment and selection costs. There were other additional costs, including:"},{"t":"li","x":["relocation costs for the senior pharmacist and deputy senior pharmacist","training costs for pharmacists who also became shop managers","training costs for delivery driver roles."]},{"t":"p","x":"A number of the staff who retained their job roles had originally been employed in the shops before the takeover by CCL. Some of these staff were not very flexible and were resistant to any change in their job role."},{"t":"p","x":"Recently Nick Cookson has refitted part of the Shirevale shop as a section selling cards, gifts and glassware. This has proven to be a success and Nick now wants to introduce similar sections into each of the 10 other shops. This development will impact on current job roles and will involve another restructuring."},{"t":"p","x":"Nick is aware of the problems associated with his last restructuring. He has contracted your management consultancy to help with the planning of this change."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager to give to the owners of Cookson Chemist Ltd (CCL). Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the need to manage the proposed change at CCL successfully, including the factors and stakeholders that will influence this change","make recommendations about the actions CCL needs to take to manage the proposed change."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in a minimum size 12-point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation for your manager to give to the owners of CCL . The presentation must be accompanied by speaker’s notes/script."},{"t":"p","x":"The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the purpose, types and impact of performance appraisals that CCL could use","recommend improvements that CCL could make in its performance appraisal processes. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"6 result in a reduction in costs. One of the other impacts of redundancy is that it can also reduce motivation. Employees often say that they like their job because of the people they work with and, if these people leave, it will impact on the motivation of those who remain."},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 2."},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 2"},{"t":"p","x":"7 Cookson Chemist Ltd (CCL) CCL is a small regional chain of chemist shops with its headquarters in Shirevale. CCL was originally set up by James and Victoria Cookson. Its shares are now owned by family and friends of Nick Cookson, the current Managing Director. Two other directors manage the company with Nick."},{"t":"p","x":"8 Nick Cookson and his two fellow directors thought that the letters sent to staff and the notices on the staff notice boards in each shop clearly communicated the reasons for the reorganisation and the new structure that was to be put in place. The restructuring process caused a period of uncertainty among staff. The staff also experienced a loss of motivation and a loss of trust in Nick Cookson and his two fellow directors. There was an increase in voluntary staff turnover including the loss of pharmacists. CCL then had unexpected recruitment and selection costs. There were other additional costs, including:"}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2001","unit":6,"key":"2020-01","series":"January 2020","title":"Lilidays Centre — concert venue","theme":"Management and leadership styles","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager to discuss with Catherine Emile. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of the ways management and leadership functions have been carried out at Lilidays Centre and the impact on achieving its business goals\n• recommend the actions to be taken by Lilidays Centre to improve the current functions of management and leadership. The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to Catherine Emile. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of how the introduction of different types of employment contract will impact on Lilidays Centre achieving its business goals\n• make recommendations about how employee engagement with business culture can be maintained and improved after new employment contracts are introduced.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Lilidays Centre Lilidays Centre is an established concert and entertainment venue, serving the local and regional community of Shirevale. The centre is owned by a local entrepreneur, Gordon Stewart. Lilidays Centre has two halls, The Hive and The Burrow. Tour promoters can hire the halls for hosting concerts, family shows, comedy and pantomimes. Lilidays Centre chooses the programme of events and shows. The centre can refuse bookings from tour promoters if it feels the show does not match with its mission and values. The centre is well-loved by many of its visitors and has a good reputation for providing an excellent customer experience."},{"t":"p","x":"The mission of Lilidays Centre is to be the entertainment hub for local residents and visitors."},{"t":"p","x":"The values of Lilidays Centre are to:"},{"t":"li","x":["work as a team","energise people","champion diversity","be customer focused","be family-friendly."]},{"t":"p","x":"Lilidays Centre’s four main goals are to: 1. provide a varied and current programme of entertainment, to appeal to different audience types with different cultural and financial needs 2. maintain excellent levels of customer satisfaction for tour promoters booking the venue and customers buying tickets 3. maintain a positive business culture with high staff satisfaction 4. achieve 5% net profit margin each year."},{"t":"p","x":"The Managing Director is Catherine Emile. The owner and staff know she is a highly organised, motivational manager. She delegates responsibility well and spends time to support staff who need more guidance. The tour promoters are very complimentary of Catherine. They appreciate her drive and determination to ensure their events run smoothly, from booking the venue to the performance taking place. Staff at Lilidays Centre are praised by the owner and tour promoters as people who are passionate about their jobs. A recent customer survey rated very high satisfaction levels on all aspects such as efficient service, friendly approach and being welcoming. Staff consistently have a positive attitude towards their work at Lilidays Centre. Customers feel ‘part of the family’ ."},{"t":"p","x":"Though the staff work hard and customer satisfaction levels are high, the centre has not made much net profit for the last three years. The centre is now only breaking even. The owner is worried that the centre will soon start making a loss. An emergency meeting has been called to discuss Lilidays Centre’s financial situation and the contributory factors."},{"t":"p","x":"Catherine understands that making any changes to employment contracts would need to be managed carefully. Catherine knows that her friend Tamjid Khan, who runs Clearway Theatre, introduced changes to employment contracts in January 2015. Clearway Theatre moved many employees on to zero-hours contracts. After these contract changes were introduced, there was an increase in absence rates amongst employees on zero-hours contracts. There was also a fall in customer satisfaction levels. Tamjid has produced three sets of data (Figures 2, 3 and 4) to show the changes that occurred to absence rates and customer satisfaction levels."},{"t":"p","x":"Figures 2 and 3 show the level of absenteeism at Clearway Theatre, by contract type, across five years (2013–2017)."},{"t":"p","x":"Clearway Theatre: Average number of days lost per worker due to sickness"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Contract type 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017","Full-time/part-time contracts 3.3 3.4 3.4 3.3 3.2","Zero-hours contracts n/a n/a 5.2 4.8 4.7","Figure 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"Clearway Theatre: Percentage of total workforce contracted days lost due to sickness"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Contract type 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017","Sickness absence rates (%)","(full-time/part-time contracts) 1.5 1.5 1.6 1.5 1.4","Sickness absence rates (%)","(zero-hours contracts) n/a n/a 2.4 2.2 2.2","Figure 3"]},{"t":"p","x":"Figure 4 provides customer satisfaction ratings at Clearway Theatre for five years 2013–2017. This is useful for Catherine because maintaining excellent levels of customer satisfaction at Lilidays Centre is one of the centre’s goals."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Clearway Theatre – Customer feedback (Average scores out of 5)","2013 2014 2015 2016 2017","Friendliness 4.2 4.2 3.7 3.8 3.8","Helpfulness 4.4 4.8 4.0 3.9 4.0","Efficient service 3.9 3.9 3.5 3.7 3.8","3.0","3.2","3.4","3.6","3.8","4.0","4.2","4.4","4.6","4.8","5.0","Figure 4"]},{"t":"p","x":"Tamjid told Catherine that he is planning to give each employee a personal rota to show the days and hours that they have to work for the next full calendar month. They will be given the rota at least two weeks before the start of the month. For instance, they will receive their December rota on 15 November. Tamjid hopes that this initiative, which is one of a number of ideas, will improve business culture and employee satisfaction at Clearway Theatre."},{"t":"p","x":"What is a tour promoter?"},{"t":"p","x":"Tour promoters (or concert promoters) are the people or companies responsible for organising live concert tours, family shows, comedy performances and pantomimes. A tour promoter offers employment to a performer, usually through the performer’s agent or manager."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager to discuss with Catherine Emile. Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the ways management and leadership functions have been carried out at Lilidays Centre and the impact on achieving its business goals","recommend the actions to be taken by Lilidays Centre to improve the current functions of management and leadership."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to Catherine Emile. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script."},{"t":"p","x":"The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of how the introduction of different types of employment contract will impact on Lilidays Centre achieving its business goals","make recommendations about how employee engagement with business culture can be maintained and improved after new employment contracts are introduced. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The values of Lilidays Centre are to:"},{"t":"p","x":"7 Catherine understands that making any changes to employment contracts would need to be managed carefully. Catherine knows that her friend Tamjid Khan, who runs Clearway Theatre, introduced changes to employment contracts in January 2015. Clearway Theatre moved many employees on to zero-hours contracts. After these contract changes were introduced, there was an increase in absence rates amongst employees on zero-hours contracts. There was also a fall in customer satisfaction levels. Tamjid has produced three sets of data (Figures 2, 3 and 4) to show the changes that occurred to absence rates and customer satisfaction levels."},{"t":"p","x":"8 Figure 4 provides customer satisfaction ratings at Clearway Theatre for five years 2013–2017. This is useful for Catherine because maintaining excellent levels of customer satisfaction at Lilidays Centre is one of the centre’s goals."},{"t":"p","x":"What is a tour promoter?"}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2101","unit":6,"key":"2021-01","series":"January 2021","title":"Clockwise Ltd","theme":"Management and leadership","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a report for the owners of Clockwise Ltd. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of management and leadership at Clockwise Ltd and its impact on staff turnover and profitability\n• recommend how management and leadership styles and skills could be changed to reduce staff turnover and increase profitability. The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a presentation for the owners of Clockwise Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of how Clockwise Ltd currently motivates its staff\n• recommend how Clockwise Ltd could improve the motivation and performance of its staff.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Clockwise Ltd Clockwise Ltd is a technology company founded by Patrick Neve and his two friends, Mitra Vinod and Mehdi Rohit. They set up the company five years ago whilst studying Computing at university. The company’s only product is a mobile social media video app for creating and sharing short lip-sync, comedy, and talent videos. Clockwise Ltd has become a global success, with more than 300 million people using the app each month. Revenue from the last 12 months exceeded £6 million."},{"t":"p","x":"Patrick, Mitra and Mehdi had no previous management experience, but they have the following roles within Clockwise Ltd: Patrick is the Financial Director, Mitra is the Logistics Director and Mehdi is the Director of Human Resources. They employ more than 50 staff in a number of job roles from software engineers to online marketing experts. Most staff are on flexible contracts with the option to work from home and work hours to suit their personal circumstances. However, the software engineers who maintain the network and the app work a shift pattern. Some of the engineers work a 12-hour day shift, and other engineers work a 12-hour night shift. All the software engineers are highly skilled and experienced. These highly-skilled software engineers are the most difficult to recruit and retain because there is a shortage of highly skilled software engineers in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"Patrick thinks this shortage of highly-skilled software engineers is holding the company back. Clockwise Ltd has had to outsource software engineering tasks to an agency, which has been very expensive. Also, Mitra’s laid-back management style often means the day shift team do not complete tasks, which the night shift team have to finish. There have been arguments because the night-shift team resent having to do this extra work without getting any extra pay for doing it. One of the night shift team commented, ‘There doesn’t seem to be a plan, no wonder everyone is leaving – we’re all overworked and undervalued. ’ Permanent staff are paid an annual salary, but agency staff earn an hourly wage at a higher rate per hour than the permanent staff. In addition, permanent staff get an annual bonus if the company exceeds its profit targets for the year. The staff do not have individual performance targets and there are not many opportunities for promotion in the company. Some staff lack motivation and leave as they feel unchallenged. Clockwise Ltd is located on an industrial park on the outskirts of one of the UK’s major cities. There is no direct bus route to the office and staff without cars have a 20-minute walk from the nearest bus stop. The difficulty in getting to the office is another reason why people do not want to work for Clockwise Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"Most employees enjoy the laid-back management style and positive relationships with the management team. However, Mehdi, the Director of Human Resources, recently had to discipline some software engineers in the recreational area because they were playing table tennis when they should have been correcting an urgent software error. This was causing problems for users worldwide and resulted in the loss of a major new advertising contract. Patrick was already worried because there was a reduction in the company’s net profit, and the loss of the advertising revenue made the problem worse. This reduction in profit is reflected in Figure 1."},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the financial information for Clockwise Ltd"},{"t":"tbl","x":["for the years 2016–2020","2016 2017 2018 2019 2020","Total revenue","(from advertising and","sponsorship)","£1 million £1.1 million £5.4 million £6 million £6.2 million"]},{"t":"p","x":"Net profit/loss after tax £150 000 £187 000 £1 080 000 £900 000 £806 000 Net profit % 15% 17% 20% 15% 13%"},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Patrick felt that a review of staffing was needed. As a result, Mehdi conducted a staffing analysis. The following information is taken from his analysis: Extract from the staffing and logistical information for Clockwise Ltd"},{"t":"tbl","x":["for the years 2016–2020","2016 2017 2018 2019 2020","Worker satisfaction survey (showing % of","staff happy and committed to their job) 63% 65% 48% 43% 41%","Staff turnover % 5% 7% 30% 35% 42%","% of total wage bill spent on outsourcing","engineering jobs to agencies 2% 2.4% 13% 24% 45%","Unfilled vacancies for which skilled workers","could not be found 1 3 6 10 13","Figure 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"In conversation with Mitra, Patrick and Mehdi agreed that staff salaries and high agency fees were the major reason for low profits but all agreed that they had to pay high salaries due to a shortage of experienced and well-qualified staff. Agency fees and outsourcing had recently become an acute problem as a rival company was offering Clockwise Ltd’s software engineers higher wages to leave Clockwise Ltd and work for them. However, Mehdi felt that their lack of management and leadership experience was also a key factor that had led to an unorganised workforce over which they had little control. Clockwise Ltd has identified the following goals for its future: 1. Increase the net profit % 2. Reduce staff turnover 3. Achieve greater levels of product innovation and creativity."},{"t":"p","x":"Mehdi wanted to solve the company’s problems, so recently he did some secondary research into the technology industry. He found that the fastest growing technology businesses in the UK are those with the most innovative product range. In 2020, total revenue in the most innovative companies grew by an average of 11.6% compared to Clockwise Ltd, which increased by only 3.3%. Mehdi visited one of these successful companies. He was impressed by the work ethic of the staff there and their commitment to an innovative and creative culture. Management teams were inspirational and they made sure that all workers were motivated towards the achievement of clear objectives. Regular appraisal meetings were used to share the company vision and to help build positive internal relationships. Staff were able to share their own personal goals, which often led to training, coaching and mentoring opportunities and greater job satisfaction. On his return from his visit, Mehdi presented the following plan to Patrick and Mitra aimed at improving net profit and employee retention, in addition to building an innovative and creative culture at Clockwise Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"Details of the plan are shown below."},{"t":"li","x":["To relocate the office to a waterfront location in the city centre, with a roof terrace and coffee bar. It will be 15% more expensive than its current location. All employees will work from the new office.","To introduce a savings scheme for staff. Staff could save a sum of money for three years and then they could buy Clockwise Ltd shares at a 50% discount on the quoted share price.","To have staff appraisals which would be held every six months and find opportunities for staff to get extra training and improve their skills."]},{"t":"p","x":"Patrick and Mitra were both impressed with Mehdi’s plan. However, they were concerned about the cost of implementation given their current net profit position. They also thought the company needed to do more."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a report for the owners of Clockwise Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of management and leadership at Clockwise Ltd and its impact on staff turnover and profitability","recommend how management and leadership styles and skills could be changed to reduce staff turnover and increase profitability."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a presentation for the owners of Clockwise Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of how Clockwise Ltd currently motivates its staff","recommend how Clockwise Ltd could improve the motivation and performance of its staff. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"6 Extract from the financial information for Clockwise Ltd"},{"t":"h","x":"Figure 1"},{"t":"p","x":"7 Mehdi wanted to solve the company’s problems, so recently he did some secondary research into the technology industry. He found that the fastest growing technology businesses in the UK are those with the most innovative product range. In 2020, total revenue in the most innovative companies grew by an average of 11.6% compared to Clockwise Ltd, which increased by only 3.3%. Mehdi visited one of these successful companies. He was impressed by the work ethic of the staff there and their commitment to an innovative and creative culture. Management teams were inspirational and they made sure that all workers were motivated towards the achievement of clear objectives. Regular appraisal meetings were used to share the company vision and to help build positive internal relationships. Staff were able to share their own personal goals, which often led to training, coaching and mentoring opportunities and greater job satisfaction. On his return from his visit, Mehdi presented the following plan to Patrick and Mitra aimed at improving net profit and employee retention, in addition to building an innovative and creative culture at Clockwise Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"Details of the plan are shown below."}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2201","unit":6,"key":"2022-01","series":"January 2022","title":"The Fachetti Group","theme":"Management and HR","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager for The Fachetti Group. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of the problems that led to Fabio permanently closing the restaurants\n• recommend how The Fachetti Group could have saved the restaurant side of the business.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to The Fachetti Group. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of how the culture of the two different divisions of The Fachetti Group may have impacted on effectively managing the change\n• recommend the actions that could be taken by the management of Fachetti’s Kitchen to help it achieve its business goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The Fachetti Group Fabio Fachetti is a celebrity chef and TV presenter. He is known for his innovative Italian recipes and collection of restaurants, Fachetti’s, in the UK’s major cities. Fabio is also famous for his ability to inspire and engage his staff. Fabio has a chain of restaurants and he also has a manufacturing operation making gourmet chilled Italian meals. He sells the meals to a number of the UK’s major supermarkets. The meals are all made to a high standard using traditional methods and ingredients. The meals are sold at a premium price under the Fachetti’s Kitchen brand."},{"t":"p","x":"The business is divided into the two distinct business divisions – restaurants and manufacturing. Each division has its own divisional manager. Katrina Szynalski is divisional manager for the restaurant division. She has 20 years’ experience as head chef in Fabio’s most successful restaurant. She also has excellent interpersonal and organisational skills and a democratic management style. Katrina believes it is essential to consult regularly with the 15 restaurant managers and head chefs so that the best decisions are made. She thinks this strategy of consultation is essential in the fast-moving restaurant industry. Staff in the restaurants are paid above the average wage for the industry to attract innovative and experienced workers, even though many of the city centre locations struggle with high rent and rates. These factors have often led to cash flow problems and caused Katrina’s management and leadership skills to be called into question."},{"t":"p","x":"By contrast Sunny Elder, who runs the manufacturing division, Fachetti’s Kitchen, is an autocratic leader. He runs his factories according to a strict set of rules and staff are rarely asked for their input. Staff are often disciplined for failing to follow these rules. Sunny thinks he is right to discipline them because he has to make sure that the factories are safe for customers and staff, and that the high quality food products are produced efficiently."},{"t":"p","x":"Katrina and Sunny often argue when they have meetings with Fabio to discuss the future direction of the business. Katrina feels Sunny’s transactional style limits his creativity and compassion for his staff. In contrast, Sunny has accused Katrina of caring too much about the opinions and needs of staff at the expense of profit and cash flow. During 2020, many restaurants were faced with business disruption and were forced to close. Restaurant owners had to look for other revenue streams. Initially Fabio opened the kitchens in his restaurants and took orders for home deliveries. This allowed him to provide continuous employment for most of his staff, although the front of house staff had to take up new roles as delivery drivers and order takers. Fabio was very pleased with the commitment and loyalty they had shown."},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the financial information for Fachetti’s restaurants for the year 2019"},{"t":"tbl","x":["March 2019 April 2019 May 2019 June 2019","Revenue £26 million £30 million £33 million £34 million","Net profit/loss £3 million £4 million £4.5 million £6 million","Wages and salaries £13 million £13 million £14.5 million £14.5 million","Cash flow closing","balance (Actual)","£2 million £6 million £8 million £9 million","Figure 1"]},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the financial information for Fachetti’s restaurants for the year 2020"},{"t":"tbl","x":["March 2020 April 2020 May 2020 June 2020","Revenue £10 million £11 million £11 million £11.5 million","Net profit/loss (£5 million) (£4 million) (£4 million) (£3.5 million)","Wages and salaries £9 million £9 million £9 million £9 million","Cash flow closing","balance (Actual)","£3 million £8 million £12 million £16 million","Figure 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"Despite performing well compared to other restaurant chains, Fabio was concerned that many of his restaurants could not survive after the closure. In July 2020 Fabio held a meeting with Katrina and Sunny to discuss permanently closing all of the restaurants with immediate effect. Fabio accepted that there were many skilled staff employed in the restaurants and that they had shown enormous commitment to the business. He suggested these staff were offered alternative employment in the factories. Katrina asked if she could meet with her team to put together a plan to save the restaurants. Fabio said that he was under pressure to make a decision quickly, but he would consider a rescue plan. He put Sunny in charge of developing the alternative plan and the final business proposal."},{"t":"p","x":"Katrina immediately organised a meeting for the 15 restaurant managers and head chefs to discuss a rescue plan, but did not invite Sunny. Yasmin, one of the head chefs, suggested that the restaurant business could be saved if Fabio allowed them to run both the restaurant and the home delivery service together. The other managers wanted to save their jobs, so they agreed to go away to consult with their teams and put some figures together for this alternative plan. When Sunny found out that Katrina and the team had held a meeting without him, he drew up a proposal for Fabio, which concluded that the restaurants were no longer viable."},{"t":"p","x":"Fabio called a meeting for all staff where he laid out the plans for the future of the business."},{"t":"li","x":["The restaurants were to close with immediate effect.","Two new factories would be opened.","Sunny was to continue as divisional manager for manufacturing.","Katrina would be demoted to deputy divisional manager for manufacturing.","Head chefs would be offered positions as quality inspectors – one quality inspector per factory.","All other restaurant staff could apply for jobs in the factories in either cooking or dispatch."]},{"t":"p","x":"Fachetti’s Kitchen manufacturing division Despite the pay in the factory being much higher than in the restaurants, many of the staff did not apply for the new roles. Fabio lost over 60% of his restaurant staff, including all his head chefs."},{"t":"p","x":"Fabio has set the following goals for Fachetti’s Kitchen: 1. Zero defect production 2. Reduce staff turnover to January 2020 levels 3. Increase net profit by 10%."},{"t":"p","x":"The chilled ready meals market is very competitive. In January 2020, Sunny had introduced a quality assurance system to improve business performance. Each team in the business, e.g. cooking, cold store, dispatch, is given its own target. The targets are monitored and the staff are then paid bonuses if the targets are met. The quality assurance system ran smoothly for six months before the redeployment of the restaurant staff. Since that time, there have been some problems. Some teams have realised that they are failing to hit their targets because other teams have not met theirs."},{"t":"p","x":"For example, the dispatch team has targets to load lorries with meals for customers, but failed to meet its ‘dispatch on time’ target in July 2020. This was because the packaging team was late delivering two large orders. This team had many of the new staff redeployed from the restaurants. When Sunny was told of this problem, his response was that if the new staff could not meet targets then they should lose their jobs. He said that the reputation and efficiency of Fachetti’s Kitchen was at risk if he let standards slip and he was not about to let that happen."},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the staffing, market and financial information for"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Fachetti’s Kitchen manufacturing division for 2020","Jan – Jun 2020 July 2020 Aug 2020 Sept 2020","Staff hitting targets and","earning bonuses","100% 60% 60% 68%","Staff turnover 12% 18% 24% 22%","Stock wastage caused by","defects/late deliveries","12% 33% 30% 28%","Market share – chilled","Italian ready meals","16% 15% 12% 13%","Net profit £76 million £10.5 million £10.5 million £11.2 million","Figure 3"]},{"t":"p","x":"In a meeting with Sunny, Katrina told him that staff needed support not threats. She suggested it would reduce conflict and resentment between staff, if each team had its own stand-alone target that did not rely on the performance of other teams. Sunny told Katrina that she didn’t understand how to run a factory and that she needed to toughen up if she was to succeed in her new role. Katrina phoned Fabio, who told her that they needed to sort it out for themselves."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for your manager for The Fachetti Group."},{"t":"p","x":"Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the problems that led to Fabio permanently closing the restaurants","recommend how The Fachetti Group could have saved the restaurant side of the business."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12 font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to The Fachetti Group. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script."},{"t":"p","x":"The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of how the culture of the two different divisions of"]},{"t":"p","x":"The Fachetti Group may have impacted on effectively managing the change"},{"t":"li","x":["recommend the actions that could be taken by the management of Fachetti’s Kitchen to help it achieve its business goals. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"7 Despite performing well compared to other restaurant chains, Fabio was concerned that many of his restaurants could not survive after the closure. In July 2020 Fabio held a meeting with Katrina and Sunny to discuss permanently closing all of the restaurants with immediate effect. Fabio accepted that there were many skilled staff employed in the restaurants and that they had shown enormous commitment to the business. He suggested these staff were offered alternative employment in the factories. Katrina asked if she could meet with her team to put together a plan to save the restaurants. Fabio said that he was under pressure to make a decision quickly, but he would consider a rescue plan. He put Sunny in charge of developing the alternative plan and the final business proposal."}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2206","unit":6,"key":"2022-06","series":"June 2022","title":"DingDong Ltd","theme":"Human resource management","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a report for the owners of DingDong Ltd. Your report should:\n• show how human resource management at DingDong Ltd has impacted on its success\n• recommend how Dingdong Ltd could improve its management of human resources.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a presentation for the owners of DingDong Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of how effectively DingDong Ltd currently manages quality\n• recommend how DingDong Ltd can improve the quality culture to support continued growth of the parcel delivery business.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"DingDong Ltd DingDong Ltd has operated as a private hire taxi company in the North West of England for 15 years. DingDong Ltd is owned and managed by Sami Akthar and her son, Mansoor. Sami has been responsible for all aspects of managing staff, with Mansoor in charge of new business and logistics. DingDong Ltd originally operated with 28 drivers who worked a combination of full-time and part-time hours. The business was open 24 hours per day and 365 days per year. However, in recent years, with increased competition in the private hire taxi market, DingDong Ltd has struggled."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2019, Mansoor identified that the UK market for parcel delivery had grown by 35% in the previous 10 years. Small independent businesses accounted for 30.3% of the total market share. The UK parcel delivery market is projected to achieve 2.3 billion parcels by 2023. After a team meeting with the drivers, DingDong Ltd diversified into parcel delivery. The largest growth area is the demand for rapid, same-day and next-day delivery. The drivers, who are used to this type of flexible working pattern, believed they were able to fit in the parcel deliveries around their other taxi driving work. Initially, Mansoor developed excellent relationships with some of the large online retailers who subcontracted their North West deliveries to DingDong Ltd. However, since neither Sami nor Mansoor had previous experience of warehousing or storage and with upwards of 1500 parcels arriving each day, storing and tracking parcels for delivery in their small office soon become chaotic. Many parcels were lost and with that came an increase in complaints from customers."},{"t":"p","x":"To help resolve these issues Sami and Mansoor have moved into a new 3,900 square foot secure warehouse from where they operate both parts of the business. They have also purchased parcel tracking technology that allows them to track all parcels until the point of delivery. Sophisticated software is used to track the location of each parcel in the warehouse and allocate them to the driver based on delivery location. The drivers are paid for each successful delivery, which makes their weekly pay unpredictable. As a result, some drivers have applied for positions in the warehouse with a fixed hourly rate of pay rather than delivering parcels as self-employed contractors. The parcel delivery market continues to grow and with its parcel tracking technology now in place, DingDong Ltd is keen to expand further. However, a shortage of reliable staff has meant that it is unable to accept new delivery contracts. To overcome this, Sami has used a combination of temporary staff, agency workers, subcontractors and employees on zero hours contracts. This can be expensive and, as a consequence, the contribution to profit of each delivery has fallen. Between spring 2020 and autumn 2021, the number of staff needed to meet existing contracts increased from 28 to 52 but DingDong Ltd often struggled to retain more than 40 staff at any one time. The drivers work independently and their only contact with Sami and Mansoor is through computer generated text messages and emails giving them instructions on where to collect and drop off parcels. Driver income is unpredictable and drivers are often expected to make a delivery within one hour. Drivers have the option to decline to make a delivery if they are unable to complete it within the required time. As a consequence, driver satisfaction rates are very low. Absenteeism, staff turnover and the number of declined orders have increased."},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the logistics and quality standards Information"},{"t":"tbl","x":["for DingDong Ltd for the years 2020–2021","Jan–Jun 2020 Jul–Dec 2020 Jan–Jun 2021 Jul–Dec 2021","Number of parcels","delivered 68 750 180 000 275 000 425 000","Parcels delivered","on time 95% 87% 82% 76%","Parcels lost/damaged 1% 3% 9% 12%","Contribution to profit of","each delivery 25% 22% 17% 9%","Figure 1"]},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the staffing Information for DingDong Ltd drivers"},{"t":"tbl","x":["for the years 2020–2021","Jan–Jun 2020 Jul–Dec 2020 Jan–Jun 2021 Jul–Dec 2021","Employee satisfaction rate 93% 95% 82% 76%","Staff turnover 4% 7% 17% 29%","Absenteeism/deliveries","declined by staff 0% 0% 12% 16%","Figure 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"Despite the parcel tracking technology many same-day and next-day deliveries were still not arriving on time and one of DingDong Ltd’s biggest customers threatened to cancel its delivery contract. To address this issue Sami and Mansoor decided to dictate the routes drivers must take and the order in which deliveries must be made. The tracking technology will alert Mansoor if drivers move away from the delivery route. Any driver who fails to achieve 95% on-time-delivery each month will lose their contracts. Mansoor emailed all drivers to inform them of the new procedures and several drivers resigned immediately. When contacted by Sami, one of the drivers admitted that because he wasn’t given enough work by DingDong Ltd he also had delivery contracts with two of its competitors. This driver prioritised his workload according to who paid the most and not by the urgency of the delivery. He also said that the new policy was inflexible and that part of the reason he accepted work from DingDong Ltd was that he had control over his own schedule."},{"t":"p","x":"Sami has approached a friend who works for BOXIT Deliveries Ltd one of the UK’s largest parcel delivery businesses for advice. She found the following:"},{"t":"li","x":["BOXIT Deliveries Ltd guarantees that all drivers are employed for at least 150 days each year","Employees earn 15% more than those in comparable jobs","A 5% bonus is paid for on-time-deliveries","There is a clear management structure and employees have personal contact with managers","Employees are set personal quality targets each month","Communication is good between drivers and management","Drivers are given their guaranteed hours a week in advance, which allows them to plan any deliveries around their other commitments","BOXIT Deliveries Ltd accepts large contracts and therefore can plan and control staffing costs efficiently","Employee satisfaction and retention is better than at similar businesses."]},{"t":"p","x":"Sami and Mansoor need to make a decision about how to manage their business in the future. They have identified the following goals for DingDong Ltd: 1. Continue to expand and consolidate growth within the parcel delivery industry 2. Increase the contribution to profit of each delivery."},{"t":"p","x":"Sami thinks that they can achieve this by changing the way they plan and motivate their staff but Mansoor also feels they need to improve communication with drivers and establish a quality culture."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a report for the owners of DingDong Ltd. Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show how human resource management at DingDong Ltd has impacted on its success","recommend how Dingdong Ltd could improve its management of human resources."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12 font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in Part A Set Task Information, you have to prepare a presentation for the owners of DingDong Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of how effectively DingDong Ltd currently manages quality","recommend how DingDong Ltd can improve the quality culture to support continued growth of the parcel delivery business. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Information"}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2301","unit":6,"key":"2023-01","series":"January 2023","title":"MF Gyms plc","theme":"Leadership, management and culture","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report on the operation of MF Gyms plc before the merger. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of how management and leadership styles and skills led to the problems at MF Gyms plc\n• recommend ways that changes in the management and leadership functions could have improved the performance of MF Gyms plc. The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Endris Hunt, Managing Director of Local Gym plc. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the human resource planning issues at Local Gym plc following the merger\n• recommend ways that Local Gym plc can plan the workforce to ensure it meets the business’s goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Multifit Gyms plc Multifit Gyms plc (MF Gyms plc) was a successful fitness brand with 20 gyms. The business was set up in the late-1990s. Over the past five years it had developed a reputation for outstanding customer service and innovative online classes."},{"t":"p","x":"The business employed 300 people within its gyms. It also had 15 head office employees who carried out support activities such as marketing, finance and human resources. All employees had to complete a supportive six-month probationary period. They were then given permanent contracts. 75% of employees were full-time and the rest of the employees worked a fixed number of hours each week."},{"t":"p","x":"Most of MF Gyms plc’s employees liked the laid-back management style and found it empowering. They liked the flexible work opportunities, for example branch administrators and personal trainers were allowed to carry out some of their duties from home. The personal trainers could also create their own client schedules and deliver some classes online. The managers trusted employees to be professional and work their contractual hours each week. They thought the employees did not need a lot of monitoring."},{"t":"p","x":"The managers organised weekly team meetings to discuss the performance of the gym. However, these meetings were usually social events and no one was ever made responsible for missed profit targets or if customer numbers declined. The staff were encouraged to make suggestions to improve the business, such as adding new classes or facilities. Making suggestions made them feel empowered and working relationships were positive. However, there was no system in place to monitor the success of these new activities or to budget for them. As a result, the activities did not often make a profit. MF Gyms plc did not conduct staff appraisals and although training and development opportunities were available to all staff, not many people used these opportunities. This lack of training often led to low customer satisfaction rates and a lack of available skilled staff to run popular classes."},{"t":"p","x":"At the end of 2020 rumours started to circulate that MF Gyms plc was going to be taken over by the UK’s leading gym brand Local Gym plc that had over 130 gyms. The management of MF Gyms plc said the takeover would not happen, but employees were still worried because Local Gym plc’s conditions of employment were significantly different. As a result, staff turnover at MF Gyms plc rose from 5% to 18% in the following 12 months."},{"t":"p","x":"In June 2021 there was a story on the front page of a national newspaper confirming the two gyms were going to merge. The enlarged business planned to trade under the Local Gym plc name."},{"t":"p","x":"MF Gyms plc’s employees were disappointed that they had not been consulted and that they found out about the merger from a newspaper story."},{"t":"p","x":"The gyms officially merged on the 1 January 2022. Endris Hunt, the Managing Director of Local Gym plc, became the Chief Executive Officer (CEO) of the enlarged business. After the merger the senior managers of MF Gyms plc either chose to leave or were made redundant. Endris has 30 years’ experience of working for Local Gym plc, starting as a fitness instructor. He is results driven, has a reputation for pushing for efficiency increases, and expects staff to follow instructions without question."},{"t":"p","x":"The new enlarged Local Gym plc considered making the following changes to staffing. There would be a small group of core workers who would have permanent contracts and 20% of MF Gyms plc’s staff would lose their jobs. The remainder of the workforce would be peripheral workers either on zero-hour contracts or would be self-employed specialists. For example, personal trainers and yoga instructors would all be self employed. Local Gym plc will use these peripheral workers to expand the choice of classes and training available to its members. However, these workers will only be employed when there is a demand. Endris believes that managing human resources in this way will help Local Gym plc become one of the most efficient gym operators in the country. The key data for the two businesses is summarised below. Please note:"},{"t":"li","x":["There is no data for MF Gyms plc for 2022 because it ceased to exist after the merger at the end of December 2021.","The Local Gym plc data for 2022 is for the new enlarged business."]},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"","c":["Key data for","MF","Gyms plc for the years","2018–2021"],"v":["2018","2019","2020","2021"]}},{"t":"tbl","x":["Net profit/loss after tax £130 000 £125 000 £65 000 £60 000","% of employees employed","on a permanent contract of","employment","100% 100% 100% 100%","Number of branches 18 18 20 20","Average number of members","per branch 350 360 320 280","% of gym members who are","very satisfied 65% 60% 55% 43%","Employees claiming to be","happy at work (%) 48% 52% 51% 52%","Figure 1","Key data for Local Gym plc for the years 2018–2022","2018 2019 2020 2021 2022","Net profit/loss after tax £410 000 £525 000 £575 000 £680 000 £760 000","% of employees employed","on a permanent contract of","employment","65% 63% 50% 45% 42%","Number of branches 115 121 127 128 150","Average number of members","per branch 680 670 710 725 675","% of gym members who are","very satisfied 72% 70% 69% 75% 78%","Employees claiming to be","happy at work (%) 20% 21% 17% 13% 14%","Figure 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"Towards the end of 2022, Endris carried out some secondary research to help him set future goals and inform workforce planning. His findings were:"},{"t":"li","x":["the market is predicted to grow by a further 5% over the next five years as people are more concerned about health and wellbeing","customers increasingly want a greater range of classes/activities","time-pressed customers demand longer opening hours so they can exercise at the times they choose","online classes are increasing in popularity – 34% of regular gym members plan to attend an online class in 2023 compared with just 4% in 2018","a growing number of people use fitness apps","another trend is boutique fitness where customers pay to attend a specific class – essentially pay as you go."]},{"t":"p","x":"After reviewing the research results, Endris set the following goals: 1. Increase net profit by 10% by the end of 2026. 2. Increase market share by 5% by offering a wider range of fitness classes. 3. Increase customer satisfaction to 70% by the end of 2026. To achieve these goals, Endris decided to close five gyms in locations where there are branches close together. He will also require all core employees to be multiskilled and to only work onsite so that he can monitor their efficiency. All workers must be available between the hours of 6 am and 11 pm, seven days per week."}],"partB":[{"t":"p","x":"4 W74394RA"},{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report on the operation of MF Gyms plc before the merger. Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of how management and leadership styles and skills led to the problems at MF Gyms plc","recommend ways that changes in the management and leadership functions could have improved the performance of MF Gyms plc."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Endris Hunt, Managing Director of Local Gym plc. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the human resource planning issues at Local Gym plc following the merger","recommend ways that Local Gym plc can plan the workforce to ensure it meets the business’s goals. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The gyms officially merged on 1 January 2022. Endris Hunt, the Managing Director of Local Gym plc, became the Chief Executive Officer (CEO) of the enlarged business. After the merger the senior managers of MF Gyms plc either chose to leave or were made redundant. Endris has 30 years’ experience of working for Local Gym plc, starting as a fitness instructor. He is results driven, has a reputation for pushing for efficiency increases, and expects staff to follow instructions without question."},{"t":"p","x":"6 W74394RA The new enlarged Local Gym plc considered making the following changes to staffing. There would be a small group of core workers who would have permanent contracts and 20% of MF Gyms plc’s staff would lose their jobs. The remainder of the workforce would be peripheral workers either on zero-hour contracts or would be self-employed specialists. For example, personal trainers and yoga instructors would all be self employed. Local Gym plc will use these peripheral workers to expand the choice of classes and training available to its members. However, these workers will only be employed when there is a demand. Endris believes that managing human resources in this way will help Local Gym plc become one of the most efficient gym operators in the country. The key data for the two businesses is summarised below. Please note:"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"","c":["7","Key data for","MF","Gyms plc for the years 2018–2021"],"v":["2018","2019","2020","2021"]}},{"t":"p","x":"8 W74394RA Towards the end of 2022, Endris carried out some secondary research to help him set future goals and inform workforce planning. His findings were:"}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2306","unit":6,"key":"2023-06","series":"June 2023","title":"Burr's Bakery","theme":"Leadership, motivation and development","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for Owen and Isaac on Burr’s Bakery. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of the current leadership and management issues at Burr’s Bakery\n• recommend how Owen and Isaac could improve leadership and management at Burr’s Bakery to ensure they achieve their stated aims. The report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to the owners of Burr’s Bakery. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the current staff development and motivational strategies that Burr’s Bakery use to manage the workforce\n• recommend the actions that could be taken by Burr’s Bakery to improve staff development and motivation to achieve their stated aims.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Burr’s Bakery Burr’s Bakery is a chain of bakeries that bakes a wide selection of bagels with a range of fillings and sells them in its speciality shops. The bakery is well known and popular and has become a go-to lunch stop for local people."},{"t":"p","x":"Beginnings Burr’s Bakery’s first shop opened its doors in Chester city centre in 2015. It was founded by Owen Burr, a trained baker. Owen had no previous management experience, but he was talented, charismatic, hardworking and had a clear vision for the business. His marketing message was, “Fresh, delicious food, served quickly with excellent customer service” . When the Chester shop initially opened it was owned solely by Owen. He was supported by a team of three trainees and seven experienced staff. Burr’s Bakery focused on one core product and that was bagels. All bagels were hand rolled, boiled and baked on site by Owen and his team. Owen was passionate about high-quality products and instilled this into his staff during their induction training. Owen was a dedicated owner and could always be found in the shop, baking or serving customers. He was hands-on with the team and led by example, which the staff respected. The aims of Burr’s Bakery are:"},{"t":"li","x":["to provide excellent quality products","to improve net profit","to expand the business."]},{"t":"p","x":"Growth Burr’s Bakery expanded by opening more shops in addition to the original bakery. As a result, the team of staff needed to run the business grew very quickly. Staff were recruited for both the shops and the bakery. Owen had secured some large corporate orders for office parties, meetings and other local events. The business was thriving, and Owen felt that he needed to capitalise on this success. In order to increase capacity, he would need to invest in a purpose-built production unit and was considering additional locations for his shops. He needed more capital to fund this expansion and turned to a venture capitalist, Isaac, who could offer the business experience and advice that Owen was lacking. Isaac invested £500 000 for a 50% share of the business. Isaac had no experience of the food industry but was the managing director of a business consultancy practice."},{"t":"p","x":"It was agreed that Isaac would be an active partner in Burr’s Bakery, taking responsibility for all recruitment, training and staff management. Owen would continue to focus on product development, marketing and new customer orders. Isaac felt that Owen was spending too much of his time in the shops, baking and serving customers. He told Owen that in order to manage the business effectively he would need to take a more strategic role and he could not do that if he was focusing on day-to-day issues in the shops. In order to focus on strategy, Owen and Isaac rented an office in the city centre and based themselves there throughout the week. Burr’s Bakery opened four other shops and a new production unit between 2018–2022 using Isaac’s investment."},{"t":"p","x":"In this period of growth, Burr’s Bakery took on more than 100 new staff. Most of these staff were young, unskilled workers, earning the National Minimum Wage for their age bracket. Staff were trained by Isaac. This training involved a one-day, off site, off-the-job training session at the offices of Burr’s Bakery. Training included the following topics: customer service, how to achieve targets by upselling and the history of the bagel. It was delivered using a training manual that Isaac talked the new staff through. The training had very little information on how to prepare the bagels, quality control or the day-to- day tasks required in the bakery."},{"t":"p","x":"Along with the new staff, Isaac introduced individual sales targets where financial rewards were available. The experienced staff in shops noticed that new staff trained by Isaac were focused only on sales and upselling. The shops, which had previously run very smoothly, started to receive a high level of customer complaints and some customers were waiting twice as long for orders to be made. Staff turnover, which had been very low, also increased. Some of the bakery’s original staff had left because of conflict over duties and targets and there was a lack of leadership. Others were concerned that the brand was becoming damaged and felt that since Owen had moved into the city centre office, he had lost touch with the business."},{"t":"p","x":"Concerned at the high levels of staff turnover, Owen suggested that a six-week mentoring programme was put in place to support new staff. He also spoke to Isaac about appointing shop managers in each shop and production supervisors in the production unit. In addition, he felt that loyal staff needed to be rewarded. Isaac disagreed and suggested that both he and Owen should visit shops each week to inspire workers and address any issues."},{"t":"p","x":"On a visit to the largest and original Chester shop, Owen found the following issues:"},{"t":"li","x":["routine jobs such as reordering stock were not done","conflict between staff as new staff were reluctant to undertake basic hygiene duties, preferring to focus on selling and targets","high levels of absenteeism and difficulty filling the weekly rota","staff not following their job description as there was no one monitoring their activities."]},{"t":"p","x":"When Isaac visited the bakeries, he could often be found shouting at members of staff when standards were not being met. This led to some very experienced staff resigning. Isaac also made several dismissals, including some well-liked and loyal staff. There was a staffroom in each bakery that staff used for breaks and lunches. Isaac had decided that this would be better used as additional storage space for the shops. He also reduced the staff discount in shops from 30% to 10%."},{"t":"p","x":"Isaac introduced a customer survey web link on all receipts and used feedback to sanction any staff identified as underperforming. There was no reward system for bakery staff who achieved positive reviews and the annual pay review was replaced with a bonus for achieving sales targets. Staff felt Isaac only cared about profit and neglected quality."},{"t":"p","x":"Burr’s Bakery struggled to keep its staff and cover its rotas. Consequently, staff were asked to cover shifts in other locations, often being expected to travel 10 miles further than their original shop. If it was not possible for staff to cover shifts, then agency staff were taken on for the day. Agency staff were given 10 minutes training at the beginning of their shift and both customer and staff satisfaction declined in each branch. When Owen challenged the use of agency staff, Isaac told him that it would keep costs down as they were not entitled to the same benefits as permanent workers."},{"t":"tbl","x":["2015–16 2017–18 2019–20 2021–22","Number of staff leaving 0 5 10 32","Total workforce 11 54 82 111","Wastage (due to poor quality) 8% 10% 16% 24%","Customer satisfaction rating 95% 90% 82% 68%","Figure 1"]},{"t":"p","x":"Labour turnover, wastage and customer satisfaction at Burr’s Bakery"},{"t":"tbl","x":["2015–16 2017–18 2019–20 2021–22","Sales revenue £730 000 £2 190 000 £2 555 000 £2 007 500","Net profit £80 000 £235 000 £255 000 £100 350","Figure 2","Financial data for Burr’s Bakery","Driffill’s Delicatessen"]},{"t":"p","x":"Owen was concerned about the declining performance of his business. He visited a similar business, Driffill’s Delicatessen, which had strong staff retention. The owner, Emma, accredits this to above average pay, achieving the Investors in People Gold Award and a strong focus on a quality culture."},{"t":"p","x":"Owen also found the following:"},{"t":"li","x":["there was a structured training programme that focused on innovation and encouraged staff creativity","all staff participated in annual performance appraisals and there was a clear management structure","there were no sales targets and Emma had a successful profit-sharing scheme in operation."]},{"t":"p","x":"Owen called an emergency meeting with Isaac to discuss his findings from the visit to Driffill’s Delicatessen. The aim of the meeting was to develop a strategic plan to improve business performance at Burr’s Bakery."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a report for Owen and Isaac on Burr’s Bakery."},{"t":"p","x":"Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the current leadership and management issues at"]},{"t":"p","x":"Burr’s Bakery"},{"t":"li","x":["recommend how Owen and Isaac could improve leadership and management at"]},{"t":"p","x":"Burr’s Bakery to ensure they achieve their stated aims."},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12 font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you are to prepare a presentation to be given to the owners of Burr’s Bakery. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script."},{"t":"p","x":"The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the current staff development and motivational strategies that Burr’s Bakery use to manage the workforce","recommend the actions that could be taken by Burr’s Bakery to improve staff development and motivation to achieve their stated aims. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"7 Burr’s Bakery struggled to keep its staff and cover its rotas. Consequently, staff were asked to cover shifts in other locations, often being expected to travel 10 miles further than their original shop. If it was not possible for staff to cover shifts, then agency staff were taken on for the day. Agency staff were given 10 minutes training at the beginning of their shift and both customer and staff satisfaction declined in each branch. When Owen challenged the use of agency staff, Isaac told him that it would keep costs down as they were not entitled to the same benefits as permanent workers."},{"t":"p","x":"Owen also found the following:"}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2401","unit":6,"key":"2024-01","series":"January 2024","title":"Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd","theme":"Motivation and performance","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd. Your report should:\n• identify the issues with motivation and performance at Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd\n• recommend ways to improve motivation and performance at Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12-point font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• identify the reasons why change is needed at Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd to ensure the future survival and success of the business\n• recommend ways that changes could be managed with reference to quality management.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd is a theme park in Worcestershire. It has been owned and managed by the Smith family for over 50 years."},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals of Saxon Fields are:"},{"t":"li","x":["to be one of the top 10 tourist attractions in the UK","to increase customer satisfaction","to increase profit."]},{"t":"p","x":"Saxon Fields is usually open from March until October each year because most of its attractions are outdoors and visitor numbers are affected by the weather. Because of the seasonal nature of the theme park, most employees are employed on temporary contracts. However, 20% of the workforce are managerial and maintenance employees who work throughout the year and are employed on full-time permanent contracts. Saxon Fields attracts a very young workforce. Most temporary employees are under 25 years old. Many of these employees are students who want summer work. Originally, the business provided on-site, low-cost accommodation for temporary employees. Another benefit was subsidised food for all employees in the staff canteen. The work was fun and living on-site provided lots of opportunities for the young employees to socialise. Chris Smith, the founder and Managing Director of Saxon Fields, retired three years ago. His sons Steven and Sam and daughter Nina now run the business. Although both Steven and Sam had over 25 years’ experience as senior directors of Saxon Fields, Chris appointed Nina as overall Managing Director. Nina had studied business at university and had management experience in other industries but she had never worked in the theme park sector. However, she was keen to put what she had learned into practice at Saxon Fields and to focus on meeting their business goals. Chris explained that although Nina was the Managing Director, they must all work as a team to ensure the continued success of the business."},{"t":"p","x":"Nina’s changes Nina took several actions in the first few months of her leadership. These actions included:"},{"t":"li","x":["outsourcing all training to a local company","restructuring the business to reduce the number of employees on full-time permanent contracts. Employees who were no longer needed in the new structure were offered redundancy payments","introducing zero-hour contracts for temporary employees and reducing their pay by 10%","selling the accommodation usually used by employees to a local property developer and removing the subsidy in the staff canteen."]},{"t":"p","x":"Nina has high expectations of her employees and is keen that they have a smile on their faces to ensure excellent customer service. However, employees in Saxon Fields work 10-hour shifts on average and are outdoors in all weather from rain to intense heat. There is not much shelter provided for employees and Nina has rejected all of her brothers’ proposals to improve working conditions. For example, a request for employee waterproofs and sunblock was refused on the basis that this would add to costs. Steven and Sam manage Human Resources, which includes recruitment and employee rotas. However, Nina is a hands-on manager and often changes the employee rotas that her brothers have prepared, without consultation. In addition, she patrols the park with checklists, which leads to staff feeling under scrutiny. Under Nina’s leadership Steven and Sam have found it difficult to attract and keep temporary employees, and there are many occasions when rides and cafes are closed because of employee shortages. Steven and Sam are concerned that the changes Nina has made will prevent them from achieving their business goals and are worried about the safety of the park when forced to operate with a limited staff."},{"t":"p","x":"Steven and Sam held a meeting for all employees and found the following:"},{"t":"li","x":["employees on zero-hour contracts had a second job, which they often chose to do instead of working at the theme park","many temporary employees were looking for new jobs because of the reduced pay","employees felt over-stretched because they struggled to cover for employees who had been made redundant. Some employees felt they were unqualified for the specialist roles they now undertook","it was less fun to work at the theme park now that there was no employee accommodation","employees spent too much time dealing with customer complaints because of rides and cafes being closed","there used to be many social activities for employees, but now there are no social activities at all","staff were concerned about the quality and safety of rides because of reduced maintenance and inspections."]},{"t":"p","x":"Also, Saxon Fields started to see a rise in customer complaints. Several of the attractions were forced to close during peak times as the shortage of maintenance employees meant that rides frequently broke down. The closure of rides resulted in unhappy customers. Some customers became angry with the employees and some customers posted videos of long queues and closed rides on social media. Viral videos had also shown employees ignoring safety checks to reduce queues and avoid confrontations with unhappy customers."},{"t":"p","x":"Nina refused to change any of her strategies, so Steven and Sam asked their dad for help. He suggested that unhappy customers might become satisfied if they were given full refunds or free day return tickets. Steven and Sam put his suggestions into action immediately. When Nina discovered that her brothers had acted without her permission, she was furious and called for an urgent meeting. At the meeting Steven and Sam presented Nina with the following data they had collected on the business."},{"t":"tbl","x":["2018–19 2020–21 2022–23","Labour turnover of temporary","employees 40% 52% 67%","Employee satisfaction 70% 55% 32%","Customer satisfaction 92% 84% 46%","Ranking amongst visitor","attractions in the UK 15th 28th 42nd","Figure 1","Human Resources data for Saxon Fields","2018–19 2020–21 2022–23","Sales revenue £14.5m £8.2m £6.1m","Net profit £2.2m £0.9m £0.4m","Figure 2","Financial data for Saxon Fields"]},{"t":"p","x":"Steven and Sam recently visited a similar theme park in the North East of England, whose ranking as a visitor attraction is 8th in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"They found the following:"},{"t":"li","x":["employees can win a “Great Work” award when customers or other members of staff feel they have gone above and beyond their normal duties","quality circles are in place, where employees can win awards for suggesting ideas to improve the business","team building and training sessions are provided for the three days before the start of the season, as well as ongoing social events and activities","the business uses its website for open and transparent communication with both employees and customers","customers are offered discounts across the park for taking part in customer satisfaction surveys","day-to-day activities are led by a leadership team, directed by a democratic CEO."]},{"t":"p","x":"However, all staff are responsible for quality in their areas. Steven and Sam told Nina that they had no confidence in her leadership, stating that she lacked experience and showed no understanding of quality management. They demanded that change needed to happen soon to save the theme park and quality needed to be at the centre of this."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["identify the issues with motivation and performance at Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd","recommend ways to improve motivation and performance at Saxon Fields Theme"]},{"t":"p","x":"Park Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12-point font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["identify the reasons why change is needed at Saxon Fields Theme Park Ltd to ensure the future survival and success of the business","recommend ways that changes could be managed with reference to quality management. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals of Saxon Fields are:"},{"t":"p","x":"They found the following:"}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-2406","unit":6,"key":"2024-06","series":"June 2024","title":"Gale Papers Ltd","theme":"Quality management and business culture","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Gale Papers Ltd. Your report should:\n• show your understanding of the current quality management issues at Gale Papers Ltd\n• recommend how Aled and Gethin could improve quality management at Gale Papers Ltd.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Gale Papers Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script. The presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the issues created by the business culture at Gale Papers Ltd\n• recommend how Aled and Gethin could improve the business culture to help them to achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Gale Papers Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"Gale Papers Ltd. is a manufacturing business in North Wales. The business operates from a factory producing a range of paper products including paper plates, napkins, table coverings and cups. Their products are sold to several large retailers across the UK. Gale Papers Ltd. was established in 2014 by two brothers, Aled and Gethin. It has the following mission statement: “To exceed customer expectations for quality products and value for money.” The business goals of Gale Papers Ltd. are:"},{"t":"li","x":["to minimise costs at each point of production","to be an excellent employer with high staff retention rates","to increase market share."]},{"t":"p","x":"The factory has a core group of permanent staff. Roles in the factory include machine operators, packers and dispatch. Their duties include monitoring the machines, counting orders and filling boxes. These roles can be repetitive, and workers are not given the opportunity to try other tasks. There are no supervisory positions because Aled and Gethin manage all the staff."},{"t":"p","x":"Last year one of their main competitors stopped trading. As a result, several big retailers started to order from Gale Papers Ltd. This increase in demand led to significant changes in the business including:"},{"t":"li","x":["buying a new warehouse to help meet demand","the introduction of a shift pattern with staff working either mornings (Monday – Friday, 6am – 2pm) or evenings (Monday – Friday, 2pm – 10pm)","the introduction of compulsory overtime shifts at the weekend as part of a standard contract. Pay at the weekends was at the same rate as the weekday shifts","staff contracts were changed without consultation. Aled and Gethin allocated staff to either morning or evening shifts. Each staff member was also allocated compulsory overtime, working one weekend in every four weeks."]},{"t":"p","x":"New roles were subcontracted out to a local employment agency. Agency staff were paid at a higher hourly rate than the permanent staff. Aled and Gethin felt that using the local employment agency gave them more flexibility in terms of managing production schedules and orders. Any agency staff who performed well over a six-month period were offered permanent contracts. Many agency staff did not accept permanent contracts because there was not much opportunity to try different jobs or get promotion. Agency staff were trained for 30 minutes using on-the-job training. Aled and Gethin felt this training was enough because their roles were unskilled. More than 30 minutes of training would be money and time wasted. Errors and mistakes were often tracked back to agency staff, causing an increase in waste when products were not made correctly."},{"t":"p","x":"There were no quality control procedures in place at Gale Papers Ltd. Staff were encouraged to check their own work and the products that they made. Aled and Gethin had not shared with staff the expected standard of quality for each stage of production. Therefore, staff did not know whether the products they produced were of acceptable quality or not. Because Aled and Gethin were the only supervisors they worked at the factory seven days a week. When mistakes were made, they quickly became angry because they were tired. They often shouted at staff."},{"t":"p","x":"Aled did some research into obtaining a recognised quality standards certification because he felt the certification would help improve the business image. Gethin thought that customers did not care about these certifications and instead wanted the lowest price."},{"t":"p","x":"Gethin focused on minimising costs. He often changed suppliers of raw materials so that he paid a lower price for them. He had no loyalty to any suppliers, so there were often issues with quality and late deliveries. Sometimes deliveries did not arrive in time to meet Aled and Gethin’s production schedule and they were forced to close the factory and send their staff home. The agency staff were unhappy when the factory was closed at short notice because they were not paid. The permanent staff were also unhappy because closing the factory often led to unscheduled overtime shifts at the weekend. As a result, many reliable staff found work elsewhere. Also, customers had to wait longer for their orders than initially quoted."},{"t":"p","x":"All staff at the factory felt they were paid below the industry rate because overtime was not paid at a higher rate. When staff requested a pay rise, Aled and Gethin immediately rejected the request. There has been a rise in labour turnover at Gale Papers Ltd. Aled held a meeting with a small group of permanent staff and gathered the following feedback:"},{"t":"li","x":["agency staff and permanent staff did not work well together because agency staff did not care about the quality of work and constantly made mistakes","shifts were enforced without consultation, and requests to change shifts were always refused. Family and lifestyle commitments were never considered","staff were not compensated for working in shift patterns or at weekends","the mission statement of Gale Papers Ltd. was not considered by the owners in the daily running of the business."]},{"t":"p","x":"Several key customers complained about late orders and poor quality. When customers complained, replacements were provided free of charge. One large retailer has threatened to cancel future orders if quality and delivery do not improve."},{"t":"tbl","x":["2021 2022 2023","Percentage of agency staff employed 5% 32% 65%","Labour turnover of permanent staff 7% 15% 40%","Absenteeism (mainly weekends) 4% 5% 24%","Employee satisfaction rating 65% 52% 30%","Figure 1"]},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the staffing information for Gale Papers Ltd."},{"t":"tbl","x":["for the years 2021–2023","2021 2022 2023","Wastage (errors in production) 5% 12% 35%","Customer satisfaction rating 98% 90% 75%","Returns (late deliveries and poor","quality)","2% 5% 19%","Figure 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the logistics and quality standards information for Gale Papers Ltd. for the years 2021–2023 Aled attended a business networking event and spoke to the managers of other local factories. He found the following:"},{"t":"li","x":["there were weekly meetings where staff were encouraged to feedback on how to improve processes and production","mental and physical health and wellbeing of staff was a priority","staff were rewarded each week for their hard work and determination","many businesses had restructured as they had grown."]},{"t":"p","x":"Aled wanted to make sure that Gale Papers Ltd. improved to keep customers happy. He realised that changes needed to be made."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Gale Papers Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"Your report should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the current quality management issues at Gale Papers Ltd","recommend how Aled and Gethin could improve quality management at Gale Papers Ltd."]},{"t":"p","x":"The report should be word processed and in size 12 font."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Gale Papers Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script."},{"t":"p","x":"The presentation should:"},{"t":"li","x":["show your understanding of the issues created by the business culture at Gale Papers Ltd","recommend how Aled and Gethin could improve the business culture to help them to achieve their goals. (Total for Activity 2 = 44 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Gale Papers Ltd."},{"t":"li","x":["agency staff and permanent workers did not work well together because agency staff did not care about the quality of work and constantly made mistakes","shifts were enforced without consultation, and requests to change shifts were always refused. Family and lifestyle commitments were never considered","staff were not compensated for working in shift patterns or at weekends","the mission statement of Gale Papers Ltd. was not considered in the daily running of the business."]},{"t":"p","x":"Extract from the staffing information for Gale Papers Ltd."}],"prep":{}},{"id":"U6-R1","unit":6,"key":"9901","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Kestrel Couriers Ltd","theme":"Quality management and business culture","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Rhys and Dana Calloway, the owners of Kestrel Couriers Ltd.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the current quality management issues at Kestrel Couriers Ltd\n• recommend how Rhys and Dana could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Rhys and Dana Calloway, the owners of Kestrel Couriers Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the issues created by the business culture and management style at Kestrel Couriers Ltd\n• recommend how Rhys and Dana could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Kestrel Couriers Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Kestrel Couriers Ltd is a parcel delivery business based in the Midlands. It was founded in 2012 by Rhys Calloway, who still runs the company with his sister, Dana. The business collects parcels from online retailers and delivers them to homes and businesses across the region. It has the following mission statement: “To deliver every parcel on time and in perfect condition.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to increase market share; to keep its reputation for reliability; and to retain experienced staff."},{"t":"p","x":"Kestrel has a core group of permanent drivers and warehouse staff. Rhys and Dana manage all staff directly and there are no team leaders. Over the past two years the business won contracts with three large online retailers and delivery volumes more than doubled. To cope with demand Kestrel opened a second depot, introduced a six-day working week and hired agency drivers through a local agency. Agency drivers were given a 20-minute briefing and shown a route map. Rhys said that additional training would “cost money and take drivers off the road”."},{"t":"p","x":"There is no system for checking parcels at the depot. Drivers are told to “do their best” and mistakes are only noticed when customers complain. Rhys often shouts at drivers in the yard when parcels are late or damaged. Dana, who handles customer complaints, has started to dread opening her emails. A recent attempt to introduce a mobile tracking app was abandoned after drivers said they had not been shown how to use it and that it was “just another way of watching us”."},{"t":"p","x":"Rhys has recently moved the company to the cheapest available packaging supplier and tyre supplier. Several deliveries have been delayed by vehicle breakdowns. Drivers have complained that they are paid by the parcel, not by the hour, so there is pressure to rush. The two retailers who account for 60% of volume have written to say that they will review their contracts if performance does not improve."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Percentage of agency drivers","4%","28%","58%"],["Labour turnover (permanent staff)","8%","17%","39%"],["Staff absence (days lost)","3%","6%","19%"],["Employee satisfaction rating","70%","55%","31%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the staffing information for Kestrel Couriers Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Parcels delivered late","3%","9%","24%"],["Parcels damaged","1%","4%","13%"],["Customer satisfaction rating","96%","85%","66%"],["Customer complaints (per month)","40","210","780"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the delivery and quality information for Kestrel Couriers Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Dana visited a rival courier that is a member of a regional quality scheme. She found that drivers met in teams every Monday to discuss problems and improvements, supervisors checked a sample of parcels every day, managers handed out “driver of the month” awards, and the company had achieved a recognised customer-service standard. The rival also had a structure of team leaders for each route area and offered flexible shift patterns. Dana returned convinced that Kestrel needed to change."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R2","unit":6,"key":"9902","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Harbour Hotels Group","theme":"Leadership, change, motivation and development","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Elaine Thornton, Chief Executive of Harbour Hotels Group.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the current leadership and management issues at Harbour Hotels Group\n• recommend how Elaine Thornton could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Elaine Thornton, Chief Executive of Harbour Hotels Group. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the problems with how change was managed at Harbour Hotels Group and the current approach to staff development and motivation\n• recommend how Elaine Thornton could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Harbour Hotels Group"},{"t":"p","x":"Harbour Hotels Group is a group of eight coastal hotels in the south west of England. It was founded in 1998 by the Thornton family and is now led by Chief Executive Elaine Thornton. The group’s mission statement is: “To give every guest a welcome they remember.”"},{"t":"p","x":"Its business goals are: to increase profit by 10% over the next two years; to improve customer review scores; and to be the best employer in the local hospitality sector."},{"t":"p","x":"Each hotel has a general manager who reports to Elaine. Managers have always been expected to follow detailed procedures set by head office on staffing, pricing and menus. Decisions are made at head office and passed down by email. In the past year Elaine introduced a new cloud booking and housekeeping system across all hotels to replace paper systems and spreadsheets. The change was announced by email three weeks before go-live. Staff received a 45-minute online video as training. Receptionists said the system was slow and housekeepers were given tablets but no time to learn them. Several experienced night receptionists resigned."},{"t":"p","x":"General managers were told to “make it work” and were not consulted about timing. Some hotels lost bookings because rooms were double-booked. At a recent meeting, one manager said the change felt “like something done to us rather than with us”. Meanwhile, a survey showed that team members at the hotels wanted more say in rotas, clear career paths and recognition for good service. The group has no performance appraisal system; pay rises are decided at head office once a year."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Staff turnover","21%","24%","36%"],["Staff satisfaction","62%","58%","39%"],["Average review score (out of 5)","4.6","4.4","3.9"],["Bookings lost through system errors","—","—","5.2%"],["Profit (as % of revenue)","14%","12%","7%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from performance information for Harbour Hotels Group 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Elaine also visited a competitor, Seagate Hotels. At Seagate, general managers help set targets and meet monthly to share ideas; staff are trained on new systems for two days before they are introduced; each employee has an annual appraisal and a development plan; and the company runs an annual “spotlight awards” evening. Seagate also introduced its new booking system gradually, hotel by hotel, with a team of “system champions” from each department. Elaine said: “We are good at running hotels, but not at managing change.”"}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R3","unit":6,"key":"9903","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Brindle Bakeries Ltd","theme":"Leadership style, motivation and teamwork","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Hector Brindle, Managing Director of Brindle Bakeries Ltd.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the current leadership and management style issues at Brindle Bakeries Ltd\n• recommend how Hector could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Hector Brindle, Managing Director of Brindle Bakeries Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the motivation and teamwork issues at Brindle Bakeries Ltd\n• recommend how Hector could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Brindle Bakeries Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Brindle Bakeries Ltd is a family-owned bakery that supplies bread and rolls to supermarkets and cafés across the north of England. It was founded by Hector Brindle’s grandfather in 1958 and employs 160 people on three production shifts. The company’s mission statement is: “Honest bread, baked with pride.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to win a new national supermarket contract; to reduce waste; and to improve employee retention."},{"t":"p","x":"Hector manages the business in a very traditional way. He makes all decisions himself, walks the factory floor each morning and expects staff to “do as they are told”. Supervisors are not allowed to change rotas or authorise overtime without his approval, and several have said they feel like “messengers”. Hector believes that “people work for the wage packet and nothing else”."},{"t":"p","x":"Production workers are paid an hourly rate set by Hector. There are no bonuses or recognition schemes, and most training is short and given on the job by whoever is available. Staff on the night shift say they rarely see managers. Team meetings were stopped three years ago because Hector said they “wasted production time”. An attempt by two supervisors to start a suggestion box was criticised by Hector as “moaning”."},{"t":"p","x":"The new supermarket contract requires the company to meet stricter quality and delivery standards and to show evidence that it consults employees. Hector has asked his daughter Imogen, the operations manager, to improve results but has not given her any authority to change working practices. Imogen says that staff have good ideas but are afraid to speak up. A recent staff survey found that most employees feel they are “not listened to” and several said they would leave if they found another job."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Labour turnover","12%","21%","34%"],["Absence rate","4%","7%","12%"],["Staff who say they feel valued","58%","41%","22%"],["Production workers who have a development plan","25%","14%","6%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the staffing information for Brindle Bakeries Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Waste (% of production)","5%","8%","13%"],["Products rejected by customers","0.8%","2.1%","4.7%"],["Overtime hours per worker per month","9","16","31"],["Productivity (loaves per labour hour)","210","198","176"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the production information for Brindle Bakeries Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Imogen visited a rival bakery that has already won a national contract. She saw that its managers held daily five-minute team briefings, shift supervisors had authority to solve problems and recognised employees’ contributions through a monthly award, and teams set their own improvement targets. The rival’s managing director explained that she tries to adapt her style: “I tell people what is needed on urgent jobs, but ask them for ideas the rest of the time.” Its waste rate was 5% and labour turnover 11%."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R4","unit":6,"key":"9904","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Castell Care Group","theme":"Staff development, communication and culture","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Dr Nia Castell, Chief Executive of Castell Care Group.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the current workforce planning and staff development issues at Castell Care Group\n• recommend how Nia could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Dr Nia Castell, Chief Executive of Castell Care Group. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the communication and organisational culture issues at Castell Care Group\n• recommend how Nia could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Castell Care Group"},{"t":"p","x":"Castell Care Group runs nine residential care homes for older people in Wales. It was founded by Dr Nia Castell, a former GP, in 2005 and employs 310 care assistants, nurses and support staff. Its mission statement is: “Dignity, comfort and care for every resident.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to maintain a good inspection rating at every home; to reduce its use of agency staff; and to open a tenth home within three years."},{"t":"p","x":"Care homes face a national shortage of staff. At Castell, most care assistants are paid slightly above the minimum wage and many leave within a year. Because of vacancies, the group relies on agency staff, who cost 40% more per hour. New employees are given a one-day induction and then shadow a colleague, but not all managers have time to supervise them."},{"t":"p","x":"There is no formal career path for care assistants. Several have said that they would like to train as senior carers or nurses but do not know how. Training is offered only when managers have time, and some staff have not had mandatory refresher training for more than two years. Nia has recently introduced a leadership programme for home managers, but few have attended because they cannot be released from shifts."},{"t":"p","x":"Communication is a growing concern. Messages from head office are sent by email, but most care assistants do not have a work email address. Handovers between shifts are rushed and notes are sometimes incomplete. In an anonymous survey, many staff said that they felt “invisible” to senior managers and that when they raise concerns “nothing happens”. Some home managers run their homes in very different ways: one is described as “warm and open”, another as “distant and strict”."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Vacancy rate","7%","13%","22%"],["Agency staff as % of care hours","9%","19%","35%"],["Care assistants leaving within 12 months of joining","24%","38%","51%"],["Staff completing refresher training on time","88%","71%","49%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the workforce information for Castell Care Group 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Resident satisfaction rating","92%","88%","79%"],["Homes rated “Good” or better","9 of 9","8 of 9","6 of 9"],["Complaints per 100 residents","3","6","11"],["Staff who agree “I know what is expected of me”","81%","64%","47%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the quality and satisfaction information for Castell Care Group 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Nia visited a smaller care group with a reputation for retaining staff. It runs a “care academy” that supports care assistants to earn qualifications while working, with a clear progression ladder from carer to senior carer to team leader. Each home holds a daily ten-minute huddle and weekly team meetings, and an elected staff forum meets directors every quarter. Its labour turnover is 18% and agency use is 6% of hours."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R5","unit":6,"key":"9905","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Ironbridge Engineering Ltd","theme":"Managing change and developing teams","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Marcus Hale, Managing Director of Ironbridge Engineering Ltd.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the issues Ironbridge Engineering Ltd has experienced in managing change\n• recommend how Marcus could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Marcus Hale, Managing Director of Ironbridge Engineering Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the team development and communication issues at Ironbridge Engineering Ltd\n• recommend how Marcus could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Ironbridge Engineering Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Ironbridge Engineering Ltd manufactures precision metal components for the automotive and aerospace industries in the West Midlands. It was established in 1984 and employs 220 people, including machinists, engineers and administrators. The company’s mission statement is: “Precision you can build on.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to introduce new computer-controlled machines (CNC) successfully; to win aerospace contracts; and to reduce errors and rework."},{"t":"p","x":"Last year, Marcus Hale decided to invest £2.4 million in modern CNC machines and a digital production-planning system. The decision was announced at a short meeting. Many experienced machinists were told that their manual machines would be “phased out within 12 months”. Some feared that they would lose their jobs and several began to look for work elsewhere. The union representative said that “we found out from the notice board”."},{"t":"p","x":"Training on the new machines was given to six selected employees, who then trained others informally. Older machinists say the new software is difficult to use and the manuals are not in plain language. Engineers complained that programmers and machinists work separately and do not share information. Two key CNC programmers have left after disagreements with their managers."},{"t":"p","x":"Marcus is a technical expert who prefers to deal with facts and figures. He says that he does not have time to “hold people’s hands” and that employees should “adapt or go”. The production manager, Priya, would like to involve staff but says she has no budget for training or team activities. Customers have begun to complain about errors and late deliveries."},{"t":"grid","head":["","Before CNC (2021)","Year 1 (2022)","Year 2 (2023)"],"rows":[["Employees who were trained on the new system","–","6%","41%"],["Labour turnover","7%","16%","29%"],["Unplanned absence","3%","6%","11%"],["Staff who agree “changes are well explained”","63%","35%","18%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the staffing information for Ironbridge Engineering Ltd"},{"t":"grid","head":["","Before CNC (2021)","Year 1 (2022)","Year 2 (2023)"],"rows":[["Scrap and rework (% of output)","2.1%","5.8%","8.9%"],["Late deliveries","4%","14%","22%"],["Machine downtime (hours per month)","120","290","180"],["Customer satisfaction rating","93%","79%","70%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the production and customer information for Ironbridge Engineering Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Priya met the manager of another engineering firm which introduced CNC successfully. The firm involved employees in choosing machines, held monthly update meetings, offered every machinist paid training before the new equipment arrived and set up mixed teams of programmers and machinists with a team leader. It also guaranteed no compulsory redundancies. Its scrap rate stayed below 3% and labour turnover rose only briefly."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R6","unit":6,"key":"9906","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Zest Digital Ltd","theme":"Delegation, structure and remote working","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Anika Rao, Founder and Managing Director of Zest Digital Ltd.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the organisational structure and delegation issues at Zest Digital Ltd\n• recommend how Anika could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Anika Rao, Founder and Managing Director of Zest Digital Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the issues created by rapid growth and remote working at Zest Digital Ltd\n• recommend how Anika could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Zest Digital Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Zest Digital Ltd is a digital marketing agency in Manchester that designs websites and runs social media campaigns for small and medium-sized businesses. Founded by Anika Rao in 2017, it has grown from three people to 64 employees. The company’s mission statement is: “Creative ideas that grow your business.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to double revenue in two years; to keep its reputation for creativity; and to hire and keep talented people."},{"t":"p","x":"Zest has always had a flat and informal structure. Everybody reports to Anika, who makes all key decisions and likes to approve every proposal before it goes to a client. As the number of clients has grown, designers and account managers wait days for decisions. Anika works 70 hours a week and says she is “exhausted”."},{"t":"p","x":"Since 2021 about half of the staff have worked from home for three or four days a week. Anika believes that remote working has “broken” the company’s creative spirit and has introduced compulsory daily video meetings that last an hour. Staff say that meetings are unfocused and that managers check on them too often. New starters report that they are unsure who to ask for help."},{"t":"p","x":"Three senior designers have left to join a competitor that offers flexible hours and clear promotion routes. Anika has just appointed a head of operations, Callum, but has not told staff what his responsibilities are. Two large clients have complained about missed deadlines."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Employees","28","45","64"],["Labour turnover","8%","19%","33%"],["Hours spent in meetings per employee per week","4","9","14"],["Staff who agree “I can make decisions in my job”","66%","44%","25%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the staffing information for Zest Digital Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Projects delivered late","6%","17%","31%"],["Average client satisfaction","94%","84%","71%"],["Clients lost in the year","2","5","11"],["Revenue per employee (£000)","71","63","52"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the performance information for Zest Digital Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Callum visited an agency of a similar size that has a clear structure of small teams, each led by a team leader with authority to approve work up to an agreed budget. Staff there meet for 15 minutes twice a week, use a shared project tool to track tasks and set their own working patterns within agreed “core hours”. Its labour turnover is 12% and 96% of projects are delivered on time."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R7","unit":6,"key":"9907","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Parkside Garden Centres Ltd","theme":"Customer service, empowerment and quality","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Joan and Dev Parkside, the owners of Parkside Garden Centres Ltd.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the customer service and quality management issues at Parkside Garden Centres Ltd\n• recommend how Joan and Dev could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Joan and Dev Parkside, the owners of Parkside Garden Centres Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the leadership and empowerment issues at Parkside Garden Centres Ltd\n• recommend how Joan and Dev could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Parkside Garden Centres Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Parkside Garden Centres Ltd is a family-owned chain of five garden centres in the East Midlands. It was founded by Joan and Dev Parkside in 1996 and employs 140 permanent and 90 seasonal staff. The mission statement is: “Help every customer grow something wonderful.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to increase sales per customer; to protect its reputation for helpful advice; and to open a sixth centre."},{"t":"p","x":"Plants and gardening products are seasonal. Each spring the garden centres recruit extra staff, who are given a half-day induction and a leaflet about the plants. Experienced staff say that seasonal employees often give wrong advice, and customers have been sold plants that are unsuitable for their gardens. Returns of plants have increased and some customers have shared complaints on social media."},{"t":"p","x":"Managers at each centre follow detailed instructions from head office about displays and prices. Staff cannot offer discounts or replace a faulty product without a manager’s signature, even if the manager is busy. One long-serving assistant said, “I know what the customer needs but I’m not allowed to fix it.” Weekly sales targets are displayed in staff rooms but few employees know how they are calculated."},{"t":"p","x":"Joan believes that quality is about “getting the details right” and has introduced a long checklist for displays that takes staff hours to complete. Dev is more relaxed and often tells staff to “use your common sense”. Staff say they receive mixed messages. A mystery shopper report found that customers were often not greeted and that plants on display were sometimes dry or damaged."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Seasonal staff given full product training","70%","42%","18%"],["Labour turnover (permanent staff)","9%","15%","27%"],["Staff who feel “trusted to make decisions”","55%","39%","24%"],["Absence rate","3%","5%","9%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the staffing information for Parkside Garden Centres Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Customer satisfaction rating","91%","83%","69%"],["Plant returns (% of plant sales)","3%","6%","11%"],["Complaints per 1,000 customers","2","5","12"],["Average spend per customer (£)","38","36","31"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the customer information for Parkside Garden Centres Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Dev visited a rival garden centre that has been named “Retailer of the Year”. All staff complete product training and carry a pocket guide; shop-floor employees may replace plants and offer small discounts without approval; managers meet staff for 10 minutes at the start of each shift to share targets and problems; and customer feedback scores are displayed weekly. Its customer satisfaction rating is 94%."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R8","unit":6,"key":"9908","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Meridian Contact Centres Ltd","theme":"Performance management, stress and motivation","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Sophie Lindqvist, Operations Director of Meridian Contact Centres Ltd.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the performance management and motivation issues at Meridian Contact Centres Ltd\n• recommend how Sophie could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Sophie Lindqvist, Operations Director of Meridian Contact Centres Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the issues caused by the leadership style and team culture at Meridian Contact Centres Ltd\n• recommend how Sophie could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Meridian Contact Centres Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Meridian Contact Centres Ltd provides customer service for energy and telecoms companies from two centres in the North East of England. It employs 520 advisers, team leaders and managers. The company’s mission statement is: “Every call is a chance to make a difference.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to retain its two largest clients; to improve customer satisfaction; and to reduce staff turnover."},{"t":"p","x":"Advisers are measured on the number of calls handled per hour, the length of each call and the number of customers who are offered extra products. Their performance is displayed on large screens and the lowest-ranked adviser each week is called to a meeting with the centre manager. Team leaders are told to “keep numbers up” and spend most of their time monitoring screens rather than coaching."},{"t":"p","x":"Advisers earn a small basic salary and a bonus for exceeding call targets. Many feel that they must rush customers off the phone and some say that they have been told to “end the call whatever the outcome”. Sickness absence for stress and anxiety has doubled in two years. A survey shows that staff feel they are treated as “numbers, not people”."},{"t":"p","x":"Sophie believes in strict control: “If you don’t measure it, you can’t manage it.” She has introduced further rules, including monitoring toilet breaks. At the same time, one of the company’s largest clients has threatened to move its business unless customer satisfaction improves."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Labour turnover","31%","47%","66%"],["Absence due to stress (days per adviser)","2.1","3.8","6.2"],["Advisers who say they enjoy their job","61%","44%","23%"],["Team leaders’ time spent coaching","35%","20%","8%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the staffing information for Meridian Contact Centres Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Average calls per adviser per hour","5.1","6.0","6.9"],["Customer satisfaction rating","86%","74%","58%"],["Calls resolved at first contact","81%","70%","54%"],["Complaints to clients per month","90","220","540"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the performance information for Meridian Contact Centres Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Sophie visited a rival centre that measures quality as well as speed: team leaders listen to calls with advisers, agree a development plan and celebrate good service. Advisers are involved in setting team targets and have a say in rota patterns; managers hold open sessions where staff can raise issues. Its labour turnover is 25% and customer satisfaction is 88%."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R9","unit":6,"key":"9909","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Tamar Boatyard Ltd","theme":"Succession, skills and health and safety culture","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Eddie Tamar, Managing Director of Tamar Boatyard Ltd.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the skills, succession and workforce planning issues at Tamar Boatyard Ltd\n• recommend how Eddie could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Eddie Tamar, Managing Director of Tamar Boatyard Ltd. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the health and safety culture and leadership issues at Tamar Boatyard Ltd\n• recommend how Eddie could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Tamar Boatyard Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Tamar Boatyard Ltd builds and repairs wooden and fibreglass leisure boats on the south Devon coast. It was started in 1973 and now employs 54 people, including boat builders, riggers and administrative staff. The company’s mission statement is: “Craftsmanship afloat for generations.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to complete a major restoration contract; to attract younger employees; and to improve safety performance."},{"t":"p","x":"Eddie Tamar took over from his father 12 years ago. He has a warm manner and a loyal workforce: many staff have worked at the yard for more than 20 years. However, he rarely plans ahead. Decisions are taken in conversations on the quayside and little is written down. Three of the most skilled wooden-boat builders will retire in the next two years and nobody has been trained to replace them."},{"t":"p","x":"The yard has not recruited an apprentice for six years because Eddie said that “young people don’t want to get their hands dirty”. The last two apprentices left after being given mainly cleaning jobs. A local college has offered to run a joint training scheme but Eddie has not replied."},{"t":"p","x":"Safety standards depend on individuals. Some experienced staff do not wear protective equipment and mock colleagues who do. There were four reportable accidents last year and an inspector has issued an improvement notice. Eddie says that safety rules are “common sense” and does not want to “turn the place into a bureaucracy”. Younger staff are reluctant to challenge older colleagues."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Employees aged over 55","31%","38%","46%"],["Employees aged under 30","14%","9%","6%"],["Apprentices in training","1","0","0"],["Skilled roles unfilled for more than 3 months","1","3","6"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the workforce information for Tamar Boatyard Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Reportable accidents","1","3","4"],["Days lost through injury","12","47","88"],["Staff who say they have had safety training this year","58%","37%","22%"],["Jobs completed on time","88%","79%","64%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the safety and production information for Tamar Boatyard Ltd 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Eddie visited a boatyard in Cornwall that was recently awarded a regional “Employer of the Year” prize. It runs a four-year apprenticeship scheme with a local college and pairs each apprentice with a mentor. Safety is led by a committee of staff from every area, with monthly toolbox talks and a rule that anyone can stop work if they see a risk. Its accident rate is one-third of Tamar’s."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U6-R10","unit":6,"key":"9910","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Opal Co-operative Stores","theme":"Democratic leadership, decision making and change","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":88,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you must prepare a report for Grace Mensah, the elected Chair of Opal Co-operative Stores.\nYour report should:\n• show your understanding of the decision-making and leadership style issues at Opal Co-operative Stores\n• recommend how Grace could improve this to help them achieve their goals.\nThe report should be word processed and in size 12 font.","kind":"report"},{"n":2,"marks":44,"text":"With reference to the information and data in the Part A Set Task Information, you need to prepare a presentation to be delivered to Grace Mensah, the elected Chair of Opal Co-operative Stores. The presentation must be accompanied by speaker notes/script.\nThe presentation should:\n• show your understanding of the issues in managing change and developing the management team at Opal Co-operative Stores\n• recommend how Grace could improve this to help them achieve their goals.","kind":"presentation"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A Set Task Information"},{"t":"h","x":"Opal Co-operative Stores"},{"t":"p","x":"Opal Co-operative Stores is a group of twelve convenience stores in Scotland owned by its 280 employees. Every employee has one vote at the annual meeting and an elected board of eight staff members oversees the senior management team. The mission statement is: “Owned by us, run for our communities.”"},{"t":"p","x":"The business goals are: to protect staff ownership; to improve profitability; and to modernise the stores."},{"t":"p","x":"Opal prides itself on democratic leadership: decisions are made after consultation with staff and the board. Store managers hold monthly meetings in which everyone can speak. However, decisions are often slow. A proposal to introduce self-checkouts took 14 months to approve because several stores wanted changes. Some managers say they are afraid to make decisions without a vote."},{"t":"p","x":"In an effort to be fair, the company applies the same rules to all stores, even though the stores’ needs differ. A store in a busy commuter town wanted to extend opening hours; it was refused because other stores had not been consulted. The company’s sales have fallen as supermarkets have opened new convenience stores nearby and local customers are choosing the cheaper option."},{"t":"p","x":"The board elected Grace Mensah, a shop-floor assistant, as chair. She is respected but has no experience of financial management. Some senior managers think the board is “too cautious” and does not understand pricing or stock control. Staff are divided: some want to modernise quickly and others fear that change will damage the co-operative values."},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Time taken to approve major decisions (weeks)","9","18","32"],["Staff who say meetings are “productive”","62%","45%","29%"],["Managers with formal management training","33%","30%","25%"],["Labour turnover","10%","13%","19%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 1: Extract from the management information for Opal Co-operative Stores 2021–2023"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2022","2023"],"rows":[["Sales (£m)","41.0","39.4","36.5"],["Gross margin","27%","25%","22%"],["Stores with out-of-stock problems (%)","10%","26%","48%"],["Customer satisfaction rating","89%","82%","73%"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Figure 2: Extract from the performance information for Opal Co-operative Stores 2021–2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Grace visited a staff-owned business that is regarded as a model of good practice. Its board sets the strategy and then delegates day-to-day decisions to managers within agreed limits. Each store manager has a budget and authority to change opening hours and stock, and the management team receives regular training. Staff are consulted through surveys and a quarterly forum but decisions are made within 4–6 weeks."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete both activities."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180}];
